{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01359","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01359","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01359","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shoumik","familyName":"Saha","name":"Saha, Shoumik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jifan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sam","familyName":"Mayers","name":"Mayers, Sam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanjay Krishna","familyName":"Gouda","name":"Gouda, Sanjay Krishna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Varun","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Varun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Breaking the Code: Security Assessment of AI Code Agents Through Systematic Jailbreaking Attacks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T18:38:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:05:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T23:34:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:15:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-30T23:04:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:24:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Code-capable large language model (LLM) agents are embedded in software engineering workflows where they can read, write, and execute code, raising \"jailbreak\" stakes beyond text-only settings. Prior evaluations emphasize refusal or harmful-text detection, leaving open whether agents compile and run malicious programs. We present JAWS-Bench (Jailbreaks Across WorkSpaces), a benchmark spanning three escalating workspace regimes mirroring attacker capability: empty (JAWS-0), single-file (JAWS-1), and multi-file (JAWS-M). We pair it with a hierarchical, executable-aware Judge Framework that tests (i) compliance, (ii) attack success, (iii) syntactic correctness, and (iv) runtime executability to measure deployable harm. Across seven LLM backends from five families, prompt-only attacks in JAWS-0 achieve 61% compliance; 58% are harmful, 52% parse, and 27% run end-to-end. In JAWS-1, compliance reaches ~100% for stronger models with a mean ASR (Attack Success Rate) ~71%; JAWS-M raises mean ASR to ~75%, with 32% runnable attack code. Wrapping an LLM in an agent increases ASR by 1.6$\\times$, by overturning initial refusals during planning and tool use. Additional evaluations with SWE-Agent and OpenAI Codex exhibit similar trends, indicating that JAWS-Bench can be reused across multiple agent frameworks. Category analyses identify which attack classes are most vulnerable and deployable, motivating execution-aware defenses and refusal-preserving agent designs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 18 figures, 8 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01359","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:47:10Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:47:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:01:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01409","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01409","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01409","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Cotti","name":"Cotti, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Idilio","familyName":"Drago","name":"Drago, Idilio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anisa","familyName":"Rula","name":"Rula, Anisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Devis","familyName":"Bianchini","name":"Bianchini, Devis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Federico","familyName":"Cerutti","name":"Cerutti, Federico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OntoLogX: Ontology-Guided Knowledge Graph Extraction from Cybersecurity Logs with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.2.6; I.2.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T19:46:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:08:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-24T19:29:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:07:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1002/aisy.202501381","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"System logs represent a valuable source of Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI), capturing attacker behaviors, exploited vulnerabilities, and traces of malicious activity. Yet their utility is often limited by lack of structure, semantic inconsistency, and fragmentation across devices and sessions. Extracting actionable CTI from logs therefore requires approaches that can reconcile noisy, heterogeneous data into coherent and interoperable representations. We introduce OntoLogX, an autonomous Artificial Intelligence (AI) agent that leverages Large Language Models (LLMs) to transform raw logs into ontology-grounded Knowledge Graphs (KGs). OntoLogX integrates a lightweight log ontology with Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) and iterative correction steps, ensuring that generated KGs are syntactically and semantically valid. Beyond event-level analysis, the system aggregates KGs into sessions and employs a LLM to predict MITRE ATT\u0026amp;CK tactics, linking low-level log evidence to higher-level adversarial objectives. We evaluate OntoLogX on both logs from a public benchmark and a real-world honeypot dataset, demonstrating robust KG generation across multiple KGs backends and accurate mapping of adversarial activity to ATT\u0026amp;CK tactics. Results highlight the benefits of retrieval and correction for precision and recall, the effectiveness of code-oriented models in structured log analysis, and the value of ontology-grounded representations for actionable CTI extraction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 2 tables, 7 listings, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01409","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:48:23Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:48:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-05T02:29:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01427","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01427","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01427","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sipeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Sipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longfei","familyName":"Yun","name":"Yun, Longfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zilong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zilong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Letian","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Letian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingbo","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Jingbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Tale of LLMs and Induced Small Proxies: Scalable Small Language Models for Knowledge Mining"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T20:06:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:08:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-15T07:21:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:31:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-04T21:01:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-08T00:09:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-30T00:32:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-31T00:14:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"At the core of Deep Research is knowledge mining, the task of extracting structured information from massive unstructured text in response to user instructions. Large language models (LLMs) excel at interpreting such instructions but are prohibitively expensive to deploy at scale, while traditional pipelines of classifiers and extractors remain efficient yet brittle and unable to generalize to new tasks. We introduce Falconer, a collaborative framework that combines the agentic reasoning of LLMs with lightweight proxy models for scalable knowledge mining. In Falconer, LLMs act as planners, decomposing user instructions into executable pipelines, and as annotators, generating supervision to train small proxies. The framework unifies classification and extraction into two atomic operations, get label and get span, enabling a single instruction-following model to replace multiple task-specific components. To evaluate the consistency between proxy models incubated by Falconer and annotations provided by humans and large models, we construct new benchmarks covering both planning and end-to-end execution. Experiments show that Falconer closely matches state-of-the-art LLMs in instruction-following accuracy while reducing inference cost by up to 90% and accelerating large-scale knowledge mining by more than 20x, offering an efficient and scalable foundation for Deep Research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code available: https://github.com/LongfeiYun17/falconer"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01427","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:48:50Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:48:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-31T02:35:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01483","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01483","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01483","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamad Al","familyName":"Mdfaa","name":"Mdfaa, Mohamad Al","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Svetlana","familyName":"Lukina","name":"Lukina, Svetlana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timur","familyName":"Akhtyamov","name":"Akhtyamov, Timur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arthur","familyName":"Nigmatzyanov","name":"Nigmatzyanov, Arthur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dmitrii","familyName":"Nalberskii","name":"Nalberskii, Dmitrii","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergey","familyName":"Zagoruyko","name":"Zagoruyko, Sergey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gonzalo","familyName":"Ferrer","name":"Ferrer, Gonzalo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spatiotemporal Knowledge Graphs as Persistent Scene Memory for Embodied Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T21:53:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:12:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-24T11:41:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-25T00:53:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-20T15:53:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-21T01:45:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language models (VLMs) demonstrate strong image-level scene understanding, but reasoning over long egocentric video remains costly: because VLMs maintain no persistent memory or explicit spatial representation, all sampled frames must be re-processed for every new query. We present VL-KnG, a training-free framework that constructs spatiotemporal knowledge graphs from monocular egocentric video, bridging fine-grained scene graphs and global topological graphs without 3D reconstruction. VL-KnG processes video in chunks, maintains persistent object identities via large language model (LLM)-based Spatiotemporal Object Association (STOA), and answers questions through Graph-Enhanced Retrieval (GER), which combines subgraph retrieval with visual grounding. Once constructed, the knowledge graph removes the need to revisit raw video at query time, decoupling query latency from video length. Across three embodied question answering benchmarks -- OpenEQA, NaVQA, and our newly introduced WalkieKnowledge -- VL-KnG achieves accuracy competitive with frontier VLMs while answering queries at substantially lower latency and with explainable, graph-grounded reasoning; it further surpasses prior persistent-representation baselines and open-weight VLMs in several settings. Deployment on a real robot demonstrates practical applicability, with query latency remaining stable as observation history grows. As a persistent, queryable scene memory, VL-KnG provides a concrete substrate for memory maintenance and knowledge updating in embodied AI agents."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01483","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:50:12Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:50:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-21T03:43:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01299","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01299","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01299","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dmitriy","familyName":"Kostunin","name":"Kostunin, Dmitriy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elisa","familyName":"Jones","name":"Jones, Elisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vladimir","familyName":"Sotnikov","name":"Sotnikov, Vladimir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valery","familyName":"Sotnikov","name":"Sotnikov, Valery","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergo","familyName":"Golovachev","name":"Golovachev, Sergo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Strube","name":"Strube, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Domain-Specific Agents for Cherenkov Telescope Array Control Software and Gamma-Ray Data Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T14:14:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:02:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-13T10:31:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-14T01:36:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-24T02:06:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-25T00:20:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present domain-adapted large language model agents designed to support Cherenkov Telescope Array operation and data analysis. The agents combine contextual knowledge with automated validation and iterative correction to produce more reliable outputs. This approach reduces manual effort, improves consistency, and helps accelerate operational and scientific workflows. The results demonstrate the potential of agentic systems as practical assistants in specialized research environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EuCAIFCon 2025 proceedings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01299","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":2,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:45:40Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:45:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-25T02:18:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01698","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01698","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01698","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seungheon","familyName":"Doh","name":"Doh, Seungheon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keunwoo","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Keunwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juhan","familyName":"Nam","name":"Nam, Juhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TalkPlay-Tools: Conversational Music Recommendation with LLM Tool Calling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T06:08:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:27:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-06T16:03:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:43:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T05:49:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:26:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-02T09:47:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-03T00:46:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While the recent developments in large language models (LLMs) have successfully enabled generative recommenders with natural language interactions, their recommendation behavior is limited, leaving other simpler yet crucial components such as metadata or attribute filtering underutilized in the system. We propose an LLM-based music recommendation system with tool calling to serve as a unified retrieval-reranking pipeline. Our system positions an LLM as an end-to-end recommendation system that interprets user intent, plans tool invocations, and orchestrates specialized components: boolean filters (SQL), sparse retrieval (BM25), dense retrieval (embedding similarity), and generative retrieval (semantic IDs). Through tool planning, the system predicts which types of tools to use, their execution order, and the arguments needed to find music matching user preferences, supporting diverse modalities while seamlessly integrating multiple database filtering methods. We demonstrate that this unified tool-calling framework achieves competitive performance across diverse recommendation scenarios by selectively employing appropriate retrieval methods based on user queries, envisioning a new paradigm for conversational music recommendation systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication at The Workshop on AI for Music, Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS-AI4Music)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01698","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:55:39Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:55:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-03T03:10:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01800","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01800","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01800","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thanh","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Thanh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tri-Tam","familyName":"La","name":"La, Tri-Tam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lam-Thu Le","familyName":"Huu","name":"Huu, Lam-Thu Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minh-Nghi","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Minh-Nghi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khanh-Van Pham","familyName":"Luu","name":"Luu, Khanh-Van Pham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"REBot: From RAG to CatRAG with Semantic Enrichment and Graph Routing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T08:40:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:33:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-29T04:40:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:13:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-01T07:45:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-02T01:46:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/978-981-95-4960-3_35","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Academic regulation advising is essential for helping students interpret and comply with institutional policies, yet building effective systems requires domain specific regulatory resources. To address this challenge, we propose REBot, an LLM enhanced advisory chatbot powered by CatRAG, a hybrid retrieval reasoning framework that integrates retrieval augmented generation with graph based reasoning. CatRAG unifies dense retrieval and graph reasoning, supported by a hierarchical, category labeled knowledge graph enriched with semantic features for domain alignment. A lightweight intent classifier routes queries to the appropriate retrieval modules, ensuring both factual accuracy and contextual depth. We construct a regulation specific dataset and evaluate REBot on classification and question answering tasks, achieving state of the art performance with an F1 score of 98.89%. Finally, we implement a web application that demonstrates the practical value of REBot in real world academic advising scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in Communications in Computer and Information Science (CCIS), Springer, 2025. DOI: 10.1007/978-981-95-4960-3_35"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01800","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-03T01:58:14Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:58:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:53:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01724","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01724","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01724","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["ICN"],"givenName":"Madina","familyName":"Bekbergenova","name":"Bekbergenova, Madina","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["ICN"],"givenName":"Lucas","familyName":"Pradi","name":"Pradi, Lucas","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["ICN"],"givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Navet","name":"Navet, Benjamin","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["ICN"],"givenName":"Emma","familyName":"Tysinger","name":"Tysinger, Emma","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["WIMMICS"],"givenName":"Franck","familyName":"Michel","name":"Michel, Franck","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["ICN"],"givenName":"Matthieu","familyName":"Feraud","name":"Feraud, Matthieu","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["ICN, WIMMICS"],"givenName":"Yousouf","familyName":"Taghzouti","name":"Taghzouti, Yousouf","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["UNIGE"],"givenName":"Yan Zhou","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yan Zhou","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["UNIGE"],"givenName":"Olivier","familyName":"Kirchhoffer","name":"Kirchhoffer, Olivier","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["SIB"],"givenName":"Florence","familyName":"Mehl","name":"Mehl, Florence","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["ICN"],"givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Legrand","name":"Legrand, Martin","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["ICN"],"givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Tao","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["SIB"],"givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Pagni","name":"Pagni, Marco","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["UNIGE"],"givenName":"Soha","familyName":"Hassoun","name":"Hassoun, Soha","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["UNIGE"],"givenName":"Jean-Luc","familyName":"Wolfender","name":"Wolfender, Jean-Luc","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["WIMMICS, Laboratoire I3S - SPARKS"],"givenName":"Wout","familyName":"Bittremieux","name":"Bittremieux, Wout","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["WIMMICS, Laboratoire I3S - SPARKS"],"givenName":"Fabien","familyName":"Gandon","name":"Gandon, Fabien","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["CNRS, UniCA, ICN"],"givenName":"Louis-Félix","familyName":"Nothias","name":"Nothias, Louis-Félix","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MetaboT: An LLM-based Multi-Agent Frameworkfor Interactive Analysis of Mass SpectrometryMetabolomics Knowledge Graphs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T07:05:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:29:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-27T07:48:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-28T00:44:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mass spectrometry-based metabolomics generates complex, high-dimensional data that holds vast potential for biological discovery but remains difficult to integrate and interpret. Knowledge graphs (KGs) unify this heterogeneous information by representing spectra, annotations, taxa, chemical classes, and biological activities as a single interoperable network; however, their practical use is limited by the steep learning curve of corresponding specialized representation and query languages. Here we introduce MetaboT, an open-source multi-agent Large Language Model (LLM) framework that translates natural-language questions into executable SPARQL queries over metabolomics knowledge graphs. MetaboT mitigates the hallucination and schema-compliance limitations of single-model approaches through a modular architecture in which specialised agents handle scope validation, entity resolution against authoritative resources, schema-aware query generation, iterative refinement, and result interpretation. We validated MetaboT on the Experimental Natural Products Knowledge Graph (ENPKG), using an expert-authored benchmark of natural-language questions paired with reference SPARQL queries, and demonstrate its ability to answer complex questions about plant--metabolite relationships and biological activities. MetaboT lowers the technical barrier for metabolomics researchers and enables semantic data mining without specialised programming expertise."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01724","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:56:21Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:56:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-28T02:53:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01833","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01833","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01833","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihao","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Zhihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinjian","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Qinjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongwei","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Zhongwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dinggen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dinggen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weida","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weida","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Towsif","familyName":"Raiyan","name":"Raiyan, Towsif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benteng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Benteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingtao","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Qingtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Ouyang","name":"Ouyang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaoda","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Chaoda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiqiang","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Zhiqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shufei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sumon","familyName":"Biswas","name":"Biswas, Sumon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Plan Then Action:High-Level Planning Guidance Reinforcement Learning for LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T09:28:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:35:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-25T18:23:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-27T00:05:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) demonstrate strong reasoning abilities via Chain-of-Thought (CoT), but their token-level generation encourages local decisions and lacks global planning, often leading to redundant or inaccurate reasoning. Existing methods, such as tree-based search and reinforcement learning (RL), attempt to address this issue but incur high computational costs and still struggle to produce reliable reasoning trajectories. To address these challenges, we propose Plan-Then-Action Enhanced Reasoning with Group Relative Policy Optimization (PTA-GRPO), a two-stage framework designed to jointly improve high-level planning and fine-grained CoT reasoning. Specifically, in the first stage, a given LLM is responsible for summarizing CoT reasoning into compact high-level guidance, which is then leveraged for supervised fine-tuning. Then, we introduce a guidance-aware reinforcement learning method that jointly optimizes the final output and the quality of guidance, enhancing reasoning effectiveness. We evaluate PTA-GRPO on ten reasoning benchmarks across mathematics and natural sciences, using five diverse base models spanning multiple data modalities. The results show that PTA-GRPO consistently delivers significant improvements across models and tasks, demonstrating strong effectiveness and generalization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages and 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01833","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:59:03Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:59:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-27T02:36:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01336","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01336","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01336","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avinash","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Avinash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sujay","familyName":"Sanghavi","name":"Sanghavi, Sujay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Poulami","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Poulami","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HiSpec: Hierarchical Speculative Decoding for LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T18:04:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:03:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-26T05:53:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-27T00:33:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speculative decoding accelerates LLM inference by using a smaller draft model to speculate tokens that a larger target model verifies. Verification is often the bottleneck (e.g. verification is $4\\times$ slower than token generation when a 3B model speculates for a 70B target model), but most prior works focus only on accelerating drafting. $\\textit{``Intermediate\"}$ verification reduces verification time by discarding inaccurate draft tokens early, but existing methods incur substantial training overheads in incorporating the intermediate verifier, increase the memory footprint to orchestrate the intermediate verification step, and compromise accuracy by relying on approximate heuristics.\n We propose $\\underline{\\textit{Hi}}\\textit{erarchical }\\underline{\\textit{Spec}}\\textit{ulative Decoding (HiSpec)}$, a framework for high-throughput speculative decoding that exploits $\\textit{early-exit (EE) models}$ for low-overhead intermediate verification. EE models allow tokens to exit early by skipping layer traversal and are explicitly trained so that hidden states at selected layers can be interpreted, making them uniquely suited for intermediate verification without drastically increasing compute and memory overheads. To improve resource-efficiency even further, we design a methodology that enables HiSpec to re-use key-value caches and hidden states between the draft, intermediate verifier, and target models. To maintain accuracy, HiSpec periodically validates the draft tokens accepted by the intermediate verifier against the target model. Our evaluations using various representative benchmarks and models show that HiSpec improves throughput by 1.28$\\times$ on average and by up to 2.01$\\times$ compared to the baseline single-layer speculation without compromising accuracy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01336","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:46:36Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:46:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-27T02:36:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01499","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01499","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01499","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Ai","name":"Ai, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqi","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Yuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Simchi-Levi","name":"Simchi-Levi, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Milind","familyName":"Tambe","name":"Tambe, Milind","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haifeng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Haifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Majority Voting: LLM Aggregation by Leveraging Higher-Order Information"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T22:21:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:12:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-19T03:44:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-20T00:30:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid progress of multi-agent large language model (LLM) reasoning, how to effectively aggregate answers from multiple LLMs has emerged as a fundamental challenge. Standard majority voting treats all answers equally, failing to consider latent heterogeneity and correlation across models. In this work, we design two new aggregation algorithms called Optimal Weight (OW) and Inverse Surprising Popularity (ISP), leveraging both first-order and second-order information. Our theoretical analysis shows these methods provably mitigate inherent limitations of majority voting under mild assumptions, leading to more reliable collective decisions. We empirically validate our algorithms on synthetic datasets, popular LLM fine-tuning benchmarks such as UltraFeedback and MMLU, and a real-world healthcare setting ARMMAN. Our algorithms consistently outperform standard baselines, establishing a robust, training-free framework for effective multi-agent LLM aggregation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted into ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01499","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:50:36Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:50:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-20T02:45:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01297","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01297","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01297","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeqi","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yeqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yucheng","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yucheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyu","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Hongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixin","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Yixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianxing","familyName":"He","name":"He, Tianxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SimCity: Multi-Agent Urban Development Simulation with Rich Interactions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T10:27:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:02:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-04T05:24:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-05T01:27:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-02T19:31:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-06T00:06:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) open new possibilities for constructing realistic and interpretable macroeconomic simulations. We present SimCity, a multi-agent framework that leverages LLMs to model an interpretable macroeconomic system with heterogeneous agents and rich interactions. Unlike classical equilibrium models that limit heterogeneity for tractability, or traditional agent-based models (ABMs) that rely on hand-crafted decision rules, SimCity enables flexible, adaptive behavior with transparent natural-language reasoning. Within SimCity, four core agent types (households, firms, a central bank, and a government) deliberate and participate in a frictional labor market, a heterogeneous goods market, and a financial market. Furthermore, a Vision-Language Model (VLM) determines the geographic placement of new firms and renders a mapped virtual city, allowing us to study both macroeconomic regularities and urban expansion dynamics within a unified environment. To evaluate the framework, we compile a checklist of canonical macroeconomic phenomena, including price elasticity of demand, Engel's Law, Okun's Law, the Phillips Curve, and the Beveridge Curve, and show that SimCity naturally reproduces these empirical patterns while remaining robust across simulation runs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"34 pages, 12 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01297","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:45:37Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:45:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-06T01:49:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01242","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01242","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01242","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seyma Yaman","familyName":"Kayadibi","name":"Kayadibi, Seyma Yaman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Redundancy-as-Masking: Formalizing the Artificial Age Score (AAS) to Model Memory Aging in Generative AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.0; H.1.2; H.1.1; H.1.0; F.4.0","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T05, 03C95, 94A17, 68Q85","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-24T02:18:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:01:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-19T00:53:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-20T00:26:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.3389/frai.2026.1732691","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Artificial intelligence is observed to age not through chronological time but through structural asymmetries in memory performance. In large language models, semantic cues such as the name of the day often remain stable across sessions, while episodic details like the sequential progression of experiment numbers tend to collapse when conversational context is reset. To capture this phenomenon, the Artificial Age Score (AAS) is introduced as a log-scaled, entropy-informed metric of memory aging derived from observable recall behavior. The score is formally proven to be well-defined, bounded, and monotonic under mild and model-agnostic assumptions, making it applicable across various tasks and domains. In its Redundancy-as-Masking formulation, the score interprets redundancy as overlapping information that reduces the penalized mass. However, in the present study, redundancy is not explicitly estimated; all reported values assume a redundancy-neutral setting (R = 0), yielding conservative upper bounds. The AAS framework was tested over a 25-day bilingual study involving ChatGPT-5, structured into stateless and persistent interaction phases. During persistent sessions, the model consistently recalled both semantic and episodic details, driving the AAS toward its theoretical minimum, indicative of structural youth. In contrast, when sessions were reset, the model preserved semantic consistency but failed to maintain episodic continuity, causing a sharp increase in the AAS and signaling structural memory aging. These findings support the utility of AAS as a theoretically grounded, task-independent diagnostic tool for evaluating memory degradation in artificial systems. The study builds on foundational concepts from von Neumann's work on automata, Shannon's theories of information and redundancy, and Turing's behavioral approach to intelligence."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"37 pages, 17 figures. Includes theoretical development and mathematical proofs of the Artificial Age Score (AAS), with empirical illustrations via ChatGPT-based memory recall experiments"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01242","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-03T01:44:17Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:44:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-20T02:37:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02286","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02286","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02286","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruohao","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Ruohao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Afshin","familyName":"Oroojlooy","name":"Oroojlooy, Afshin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roshan","familyName":"Sridhar","name":"Sridhar, Roshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miguel","familyName":"Ballesteros","name":"Ballesteros, Miguel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alan","familyName":"Ritter","name":"Ritter, Alan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Roth","name":"Roth, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tree-based Dialogue Reinforced Policy Optimization for Red-Teaming Attacks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:57:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:03:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-09T17:54:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T02:30:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite recent rapid progress in AI safety, current large language models remain vulnerable to adversarial attacks in multi-turn interaction settings, where attackers strategically adapt their prompts across conversation turns and pose a more critical yet realistic challenge. Existing approaches that discover safety vulnerabilities either rely on manual red-teaming with human experts or employ automated methods using pre-defined templates and human-curated attack data, with most focusing on single-turn attacks. However, these methods did not explore the vast space of possible multi-turn attacks, failing to consider novel attack trajectories that emerge from complex dialogue dynamics and strategic conversation planning. This gap is particularly critical given recent findings that LLMs exhibit significantly higher vulnerability to multi-turn attacks compared to single-turn attacks. We propose DialTree, an on-policy reinforcement learning framework integrated with tree search that autonomously discovers diverse multi-turn attack strategies by treating the dialogue as a sequential decision-making problem, enabling systematic exploration without manually curated data. Through extensive experiments, our approach not only achieves more than 44.2% higher ASR across 12 target models compared to previous state-of-the-art approaches, but also effectively uncovers new attack strategies by learning optimal dialogue policies that maximize attack success across multiple turns."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02286","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:10:19Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:10:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:50:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01674","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01674","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01674","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"He","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, He","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anzhou","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Anzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FOR-Prompting: From Objection to Revision via an Asymmetric Prompting Protocol"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T04:57:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:25:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-06T23:39:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:18:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning protocols such as Chain of Thought (CoT) and Tree of Thought (ToT) organize internal deliberation but lack an explicit mechanism for external questioning that elicits self-revision. We present FOR-Prompting (From Objection to Revision Prompting), an asymmetric protocol where a Defender proposes an answer, an Debater (Questioner) raises question-style objections with no direct fixes, and a Host optionally synthesizes the final output. Across GSM8K, FOR-Prompting matches the accuracy of CoT and consistently improves over single-prompting when evaluated under identical model backbones. On small-scale open-source models (e.g., LLaMA-3.2-1B), FOR-Prompting yields substantial gains over direct prompting and performs comparably to lightweight reasoning baselines, highlighting its promise for low-resource and on-device settings. Cross-model role-swapping further shows that performance is primarily determined by the Defender, enabling small models to act effectively as Questioners. Beyond structured math tasks, FOR-Prompting supports refinement in open-ended and multi-stage tasks: qualitative analysis shows improved exploration, coverage, and specificity, and a blind study of human preferences found that participants preferred FOR-Prompting outputs over strong LLM baselines in an itinerary-planning scenario. The protocol is model-agnostic and operates purely through role-structured prompting, requiring no training, access to model internals, or symmetrically strong agents. FOR-Prompting therefore enables scalable study of objection-driven reasoning and offers a practical mechanism for automated iterative refinement across both hosted and local LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01674","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:55:03Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:55:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:50:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01235","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01235","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01235","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subham","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Subham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhishek","familyName":"Tewari","name":"Tewari, Abhishek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Automated Extraction of Material Properties using LLM-based AI Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-23T18:27:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:42:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.commatsci.2026.114521","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid discovery of materials is constrained by the lack of large, machine-readable datasets that couple performance metrics with structural context. Existing databases are either small, manually curated, or biased toward first principles results, leaving experimental literature underexploited. We present an agentic, large language model (LLM)-driven workflow that autonomously extracts thermoelectric and structural-properties from about 10,000 full-text scientific articles. The pipeline integrates dynamic token allocation, zeroshot multi-agent extraction, and conditional table parsing to balance accuracy against computational cost. Benchmarking on 50 curated papers shows that GPT-4.1 achieves the highest accuracy (F1 = 0.91 for thermoelectric properties and 0.82 for structural fields), while GPT-4.1 Mini delivers nearly comparable performance (F1 = 0.89 and 0.81) at a fraction of the cost, enabling practical large scale deployment. Applying this workflow, we curated 27,822 temperature resolved property records with normalized units, spanning figure of merit (ZT), Seebeck coefficient, conductivity, resistivity, power factor, and thermal conductivity, together with structural attributes such as crystal class, space group, and doping strategy. Dataset analysis reproduces known thermoelectric trends, such as the superior performance of alloys over oxides and the advantage of p-type doping, while also surfacing broader structure-property correlations. To facilitate community access, we release an interactive web explorer with semantic filters, numeric queries, and CSV export. This study delivers the largest LLM-curated thermoelectric dataset to date, provides a reproducible and cost-profiled extraction pipeline, and establishes a foundation for scalable, data-driven materials discovery beyond thermoelectrics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01235","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-03T01:44:07Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:44:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:50:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02243","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02243","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02243","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linh The","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Linh The","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dung Ngoc","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Dung Ngoc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Van-Cuong","familyName":"Pham","name":"Pham, Van-Cuong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoang","familyName":"Ngo","name":"Ngo, Hoang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dat Quoc","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Dat Quoc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AccurateRAG: A Framework for Building Accurate Retrieval-Augmented Question-Answering Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:30:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:01:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T15:31:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:58:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce AccurateRAG -- a novel framework for constructing high-performance question-answering applications based on retrieval-augmented generation (RAG). Our framework offers a pipeline for development efficiency with tools for raw dataset processing, fine-tuning data generation, text embedding \u0026amp; LLM fine-tuning, output evaluation, and building RAG systems locally. Experimental results show that our framework outperforms previous strong baselines and obtains new state-of-the-art question-answering performance on benchmark datasets."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to LREC 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02243","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:09:14Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:09:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:18:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02219","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02219","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02219","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linh","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Linh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yulong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Radu","familyName":"Florian","name":"Florian, Radu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Contrastive Retrieval Heads Improve Attention-Based Re-Ranking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:03:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:59:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T00:44:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:15:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The strong zero-shot and long-context capabilities of recent Large Language Models (LLMs) have paved the way for highly effective re-ranking systems. Attention-based re-rankers leverage attention weights from transformer heads to produce relevance scores, but not all heads are created equally: many contribute noise and redundancy, thus limiting performance. To address this, we introduce CoRe heads, a small set of retrieval heads identified via a contrastive scoring metric that explicitly rewards high attention heads that correlate with relevant documents, while downplaying nodes with higher attention that correlate with irrelevant documents. This relative ranking criterion isolates the most discriminative heads for re-ranking and yields a state-of-the-art list-wise re-ranker. Extensive experiments with three LLMs show that aggregated signals from CoRe heads, constituting less than 1% of all heads, substantially improve re-ranking accuracy over strong baselines. We further find that CoRe heads are concentrated in middle layers, and pruning the computation of final 50% of model layers preserves accuracy while significantly reducing inference time and memory usage."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02219","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:08:39Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:08:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:18:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02209","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02209","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02209","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanxu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yanxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijun","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Zijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yantao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yantao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amy","familyName":"Xin","name":"Xin, Amy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianing","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jianing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juanzi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Juanzi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StockBench: Can LLM Agents Trade Stocks Profitably In Real-world Markets?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T16:54:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:59:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T14:50:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T03:13:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) demonstrate strong potential as autonomous agents, with promising capabilities in reasoning, tool use, and sequential decision-making. While prior benchmarks have evaluated LLM agents in various domains, the financial domain remains underexplored, despite its significant economic value and complex reasoning requirements. Most existing financial benchmarks focus on static question-answering, failing to capture the dynamics of real-market trading. To address this gap, we introduce STOCKBENCH, a contamination-free benchmark designed to evaluate LLM agents in realistic, multi-month stock trading environments. Agents receive daily market signals -- including prices, fundamentals, and news -- and make sequential buy, sell, or hold decisions. Performance is measured using financial metrics such as cumulative return, maximum drawdown, and the Sortino ratio, capturing both profitability and risk management. We evaluate a wide range of state-of-the-art proprietary and open-source LLMs. Surprisingly, most models struggle to outperform the simple buy-and-hold baseline, while some models demonstrate the potential to achieve higher returns and stronger risk management. These findings highlight both the challenges and opportunities of LLM-based trading agents, showing that strong performance on static financial question-answering do not necessarily translate into effective trading behavior. We release STOCKBENCH as an open-source benchmark to enable future research on LLM-driven financial agents."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02209","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:08:24Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:08:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02276","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02276","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02276","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenqi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chenqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timothy","familyName":"Denison","name":"Denison, Timothy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tingting","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Tingting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BioX-Bridge: Model Bridging for Unsupervised Cross-Modal Knowledge Transfer across Biosignals"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:51:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:02:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T16:08:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:57:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Biosignals offer valuable insights into the physiological states of the human body. Although biosignal modalities differ in functionality, signal fidelity, sensor comfort, and cost, they are often intercorrelated, reflecting the holistic and interconnected nature of human physiology. This opens up the possibility of performing the same tasks using alternative biosignal modalities, thereby improving the accessibility, usability, and adaptability of health monitoring systems. However, the limited availability of large labeled datasets presents challenges for training models tailored to specific tasks and modalities of interest. Unsupervised cross-modal knowledge transfer offers a promising solution by leveraging knowledge from an existing modality to support model training for a new modality. Existing methods are typically based on knowledge distillation, which requires running a teacher model alongside student model training, resulting in high computational and memory overhead. This challenge is further exacerbated by the recent development of foundation models that demonstrate superior performance and generalization across tasks at the cost of large model sizes. To this end, we explore a new framework for unsupervised cross-modal knowledge transfer of biosignals by training a lightweight bridge network to align the intermediate representations and enable information flow between foundation models and across modalities. Specifically, we introduce an efficient strategy for selecting alignment positions where the bridge should be constructed, along with a flexible prototype network as the bridge architecture. Extensive experiments across multiple biosignal modalities, tasks, and datasets show that BioX-Bridge reduces the number of trainable parameters by 88--99\\% while maintaining or even improving transfer performance compared to state-of-the-art methods."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02276","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:10:04Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:10:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T03:09:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01650","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01650","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01650","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kwanhee","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Kwanhee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyeondo","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Hyeondo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongyeop","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Dongyeop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Alistarh","name":"Alistarh, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Namhoon","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Namhoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Unseen Frontier: Pushing the Limits of LLM Sparsity with Surrogate-Free ADMM"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T04:10:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:24:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-23T08:37:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T02:15:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural network pruning is a promising technique to mitigate the excessive computational and memory requirements of large language models (LLMs). Despite its promise, however, progress in this area has diminished, as conventional methods are seemingly unable to surpass moderate sparsity levels (50-60%) without severely degrading model accuracy. This work breaks through the current impasse, presenting a principled and effective method called $\\texttt{Elsa}$, which achieves extreme sparsity levels of up to 90% while retaining high model fidelity. This is done by identifying several limitations in current practice, all of which can be traced back to their reliance on a surrogate objective formulation. $\\texttt{Elsa}$ tackles this issue directly and effectively via standard and well-established constrained optimization techniques based on ADMM. Our extensive experiments across a wide range of models and scales show that $\\texttt{Elsa}$ achieves substantial improvements over existing methods; e.g., it achieves 7.8$\\times$ less perplexity than the best existing method on LLaMA-2-7B at 90% sparsity. Moreover, we show that $\\texttt{Elsa}$ remains stable even at extreme sparsity (e.g., 95\\%), yielding up to $\\times$3.98 inference speedup and $\\times$7.80 memory compression over its dense counterpart. We also present $\\texttt{Elsa}_{-L}$, a quantized variant that scales to extremely large models (27B), and establish its theoretical convergence guarantees.These results highlight meaningful progress in advancing the frontier of LLM sparsity, while promising that significant opportunities for further advancement may remain in directions that have so far attracted limited exploration."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01650","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:54:23Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:54:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01879","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01879","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01879","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yisu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yisu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyuan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Haoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjie","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Wenjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaozheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chaozheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuming","familyName":"Ran","name":"Ran, Xuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"REPAIR: Robust Editing via Progressive Adaptive Intervention and Reintegration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T10:35:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:38:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-10T05:25:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-11T01:27:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Post-training for large language models (LLMs) is constrained by the high cost of acquiring new knowledge or correcting errors and by the unintended side effects that frequently arise from retraining. To address these issues, we introduce REPAIR (Robust Editing via Progressive Adaptive Intervention and Reintegration), a lifelong editing framework designed to support precise and low-cost model updates while preserving non-target knowledge. REPAIR mitigates the instability and conflicts of large-scale sequential edits through a closed-loop feedback mechanism coupled with dynamic memory management. Furthermore, by incorporating frequent knowledge fusion and enforcing strong locality guards, REPAIR effectively addresses the shortcomings of traditional distribution-agnostic approaches that often overlook unintended ripple effects. Our experiments demonstrate that REPAIR boosts editing accuracy by 10%-30% across multiple model families and significantly reduces knowledge forgetting. This work introduces a robust framework for developing reliable, scalable, and continually evolving LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01879","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:00:11Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:00:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-11T03:28:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01474","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01474","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01474","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bill","familyName":"Marino","name":"Marino, Bill","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rosco","familyName":"Hunter","name":"Hunter, Rosco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christoph","familyName":"Schnabl","name":"Schnabl, Christoph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zubair","familyName":"Jamali","name":"Jamali, Zubair","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marinos Emmanouil","familyName":"Kalpakos","name":"Kalpakos, Marinos Emmanouil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mudra","familyName":"Kashyap","name":"Kashyap, Mudra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isaiah","familyName":"Hinton","name":"Hinton, Isaiah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexa","familyName":"Hanson","name":"Hanson, Alexa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maahum","familyName":"Nazir","name":"Nazir, Maahum","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felix","familyName":"Steffek","name":"Steffek, Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongkai","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Hongkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas D.","familyName":"Lane","name":"Lane, Nicholas D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AIReg-Bench: Benchmarking Language Models That Assess AI Regulation Compliance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T21:33:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:11:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T01:10:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:20:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-07T01:41:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-10T01:19:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As governments move to regulate AI, there is growing interest in using Large Language Models (LLMs) to assess whether or not an AI system complies with a given AI Regulation (AIR). However, there is presently no way to benchmark the performance of LLMs at this task. To fill this void, we introduce AIReg-Bench: the first open benchmark dataset designed to test how well LLMs can assess compliance with the EU AI Act (AIA). We created this dataset through a two-step process: (1) by prompting an LLM with carefully structured instructions, we generated 120 technical documentation excerpts (samples), each depicting a fictional, albeit plausible, AI system -- of the kind an AI provider might produce to demonstrate their compliance with AIR; (2) legal experts then reviewed and annotated each sample to indicate whether, and in what way, the AI system described therein violates specific Articles of the AIA. The resulting dataset, together with our evaluation of whether frontier LLMs can reproduce the experts' compliance labels, provides a starting point to understand the opportunities and limitations of LLM-based AIR compliance assessment tools and establishes a benchmark against which subsequent LLMs can be compared. The dataset and evaluation code are available at https://github.com/camlsys/aireg-bench."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01474","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:49:59Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:50:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-10T05:21:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01220","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01220","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01220","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bonaventure F. P.","familyName":"Dossou","name":"Dossou, Bonaventure F. P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henri","familyName":"Aïdasso","name":"Aïdasso, Henri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Open-Ended Discovery for Low-Resource NLP"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T01:19:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:00:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-08T23:01:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T02:20:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2025.uncertainlp-main.24","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Natural Language Processing (NLP) for low-resource languages remains fundamentally constrained by the lack of textual corpora, standardized orthographies, and scalable annotation pipelines. While recent advances in large language models have improved cross-lingual transfer, they remain inaccessible to underrepresented communities due to their reliance on massive, pre-collected data and centralized infrastructure. In this position paper, we argue for a paradigm shift toward open-ended, interactive language discovery, where AI systems learn new languages dynamically through dialogue rather than static datasets. We contend that the future of language technology, particularly for low-resource and under-documented languages, must move beyond static data collection pipelines toward interactive, uncertainty-driven discovery, where learning emerges dynamically from human-machine collaboration instead of being limited to pre-existing datasets. We propose a framework grounded in joint human-machine uncertainty, combining epistemic uncertainty from the model with hesitation cues and confidence signals from human speakers to guide interaction, query selection, and memory retention. This paper is a call to action: we advocate a rethinking of how AI engages with human knowledge in under-documented languages, moving from extractive data collection toward participatory, co-adaptive learning processes that respect and empower communities while discovering and preserving the world's linguistic diversity. This vision aligns with principles of human-centered AI, emphasizing interactive, cooperative model building between AI systems and speakers."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Proceedings of the 2nd Workshop on Uncertainty-Aware NLP (UncertaiNLP) at EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01220","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-03T01:43:45Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:43:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-10T05:21:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01285","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01285","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01285","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alireza","familyName":"Salemi","name":"Salemi, Alireza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mihir","familyName":"Parmar","name":"Parmar, Mihir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Palash","familyName":"Goyal","name":"Goyal, Palash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwen","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinsung","familyName":"Yoon","name":"Yoon, Jinsung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamed","familyName":"Zamani","name":"Zamani, Hamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomas","familyName":"Pfister","name":"Pfister, Tomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid","familyName":"Palangi","name":"Palangi, Hamid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Based Multi-Agent Blackboard System for Information Discovery in Data Science"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T22:34:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:02:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-31T23:30:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T01:55:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Advances in large language models (LLMs) have created new opportunities in data science, but their deployment is often limited by the challenge of finding relevant data in large data lakes. Existing methods struggle with this: both single- and multi-agent systems are quickly overwhelmed by large, heterogeneous files, and master-slave multi-agent systems rely on a rigid central controller that requires precise knowledge of each sub-agent's capabilities, which is not possible in large-scale settings where the main agent lacks full observability over sub-agents' knowledge and competencies. We propose a novel multi-agent paradigm inspired by the blackboard architecture for traditional AI models. In our framework, a central agent posts requests to a shared blackboard, and autonomous subordinate agents - either responsible for a partition of the data lake or retrieval from the web - volunteer to respond based on their capabilities. This design improves scalability and flexibility by removing the need for a central coordinator to know each agent's expertise or internal knowledge. We evaluate the approach on three benchmarks that require data discovery: KramaBench and modified versions of DSBench and DA-Code. Results show that the blackboard architecture substantially outperforms strong baselines, achieving 13%-57% relative improvements in end-to-end success and up to a 9% relative gain in data discovery F1 over the best baseline."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01285","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:45:20Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:45:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-03T05:40:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01250","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01250","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01250","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Trung Duc Anh","familyName":"Dang","name":"Dang, Trung Duc Anh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ferdinando Pio","familyName":"D'Elia","name":"D'Elia, Ferdinando Pio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GemDetox at TextDetox CLEF 2025: Enhancing a Massively Multilingual Model for Text Detoxification on Low-resource Languages"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-24T10:06:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-03T01:21:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As social-media platforms emerge and evolve faster than the regulations meant to oversee them, automated detoxification might serve as a timely tool for moderators to enforce safe discourse at scale. We here describe our submission to the PAN 2025 Multilingual Text Detoxification Challenge, which rewrites toxic single-sentence inputs into neutral paraphrases across 15 typologically diverse languages. Building on a 12B-parameter Gemma-3 multilingual transformer, we apply parameter-efficient LoRA SFT fine-tuning and prompting techniques like few-shot and Chain-of-Thought. Our multilingual training corpus combines 3,600 human-authored parallel pairs, 21,600 machine-translated synthetic pairs, and model-generated pairs filtered by Jaccard thresholds. At inference, inputs are enriched with three LaBSE-retrieved neighbors and explicit toxic-span annotations. Evaluated via Style Transfer Accuracy, LaBSE-based semantic preservation, and xCOMET fluency, our system ranks first on high-resource and low-resource languages. Ablations show +0.081 joint score increase from few-shot examples and +0.088 from basic CoT prompting. ANOVA analysis identifies language resource status as the strongest predictor of performance ($η^2$ = 0.667, p \u0026lt; 0.01)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01250","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:44:29Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:44:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-03T05:40:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02091","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02091","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02091","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyuan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Keyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"PengXiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, PengXiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiwei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Demystifying the Roles of LLM Layers in Retrieval, Knowledge, and Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T14:57:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:52:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-30T13:22:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:46:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-31T19:28:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-04T01:07:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-27T18:53:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01-28T02:09:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent studies suggest that the deeper layers of Large Language Models (LLMs) contribute little to representation learning and can often be removed without significant performance loss. However, such claims are typically drawn from narrow evaluations and may overlook important aspects of model behavior. In this work, we present a systematic study of depth utilization across diverse dimensions, including evaluation protocols, task categories, and model architectures. Our analysis confirms that very deep layers are generally less effective than earlier ones, but their contributions vary substantially with the evaluation setting. Under likelihood-based metrics without generation, pruning most layers preserves performance, with only the initial few being critical. By contrast, generation-based evaluation uncovers indispensable roles for middle and deeper layers in enabling reasoning and maintaining long-range coherence. We further find that knowledge and retrieval are concentrated in shallow components, whereas reasoning accuracy relies heavily on deeper layers -- yet can be reshaped through distillation. These results highlight that depth usage in LLMs is highly heterogeneous and context-dependent, underscoring the need for task-, metric-, and model-aware perspectives in both interpreting and compressing large models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICASSP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02091","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:05:28Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:05:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-28T03:24:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02212","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02212","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02212","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanyang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Hanyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dawen","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Dawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenpin","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Wenpin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathan","familyName":"Kallus","name":"Kallus, Nathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DiFFPO: Training Diffusion LLMs to Reason Fast and Furious via Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T16:57:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:59:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-10T10:25:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T01:23:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose DiFFPO, Diffusion Fast and Furious Policy Optimization, a unified framework for training masked diffusion large language models (dLLMs) to reason not only better (furious), but also faster via reinforcement learning (RL). We first unify the existing baseline approach such as d1 by proposing to train surrogate policies via off-policy RL, whose likelihood is much more tractable as an approximation to the true dLLM policy. This naturally motivates a more accurate and informative two-stage likelihood approximation combined with importance sampling correction, which leads to generalized RL algorithms with better sample efficiency and superior task performance. Second, we propose a new direction of joint training efficient samplers/controllers of dLLMs policy. Via RL, we incentivize dLLMs' natural multi-token prediction capabilities by letting the model learn to adaptively allocate an inference threshold for each prompt. By jointly training the sampler, we yield better accuracies with lower number of function evaluations (NFEs) compared to training the model only, obtaining the best performance in improving the Pareto frontier of the inference-time compute of dLLMs. We showcase the effectiveness of our pipeline by training open source large diffusion language models over benchmark math and planning tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02212","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:08:29Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:08:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:26:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01869","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01869","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01869","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Nazzari","name":"Nazzari, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roberto","familyName":"Rubinacci","name":"Rubinacci, Roberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Lovera","name":"Lovera, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TACOS: Task Agnostic COordinator of a multi-drone System"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T10:21:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:38:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-18T13:11:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-19T01:44:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When a single pilot is responsible for managing a multi-drone system, the task may demand varying levels of autonomy, from direct control of individual UAVs, to group-level coordination, to fully autonomous swarm behaviors for accomplishing high-level tasks. Enabling such flexible interaction requires a framework that supports multiple modes of shared autonomy. As language models continue to improve in reasoning and planning, they provide a natural foundation for such systems, reducing pilot workload by enabling high-level task delegation through intuitive, language-based interfaces. In this paper we present TACOS (Task-Agnostic COordinator of a multi-drone System), a unified framework that enables high-level natural language control of multi-UAV systems through Large Language Models (LLMs). TACOS integrates three key capabilities into a single architecture: a one-to-many natural language interface for intuitive user interaction, an intelligent coordinator for translating user intent into structured task plans, and an autonomous agent that executes plans interacting with the real world. TACOS allows a LLM to interact with a library of executable APIs, bridging semantic reasoning with real-time multi-robot coordination. We demonstrate the system on a real-world multi-drone system, and conduct an ablation study to assess the contribution of each module."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 9 figures, submitted to the IEEE for possible publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01869","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:59:56Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:59:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-19T03:17:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01523","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01523","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01523","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shreeranjani","familyName":"Srirangamsridharan","name":"Srirangamsridharan, Shreeranjani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Abavisani","name":"Abavisani, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reza Yousefi","familyName":"Maragheh","name":"Maragheh, Reza Yousefi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramin","familyName":"Giahi","name":"Giahi, Ramin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sushant","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Sushant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MetaSynth: Multi-Agent Metadata Generation from Implicit Feedback in Black-Box Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T23:41:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:14:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-04T05:38:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-05T01:21:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Meta titles and descriptions strongly shape engagement in search and recommendation platforms, yet optimizing them remains challenging. Search engine ranking models are black box environments, explicit labels are unavailable, and feedback such as click-through rate (CTR) arrives only post-deployment. Existing template, LLM, and retrieval-augmented approaches either lack diversity, hallucinate attributes, or ignore whether candidate phrasing has historically succeeded in ranking. This leaves a gap in directly leveraging implicit signals from observable outcomes. We introduce MetaSynth, a multi-agent retrieval-augmented generation framework that learns from implicit search feedback. MetaSynth builds an exemplar library from top-ranked results, generates candidate snippets conditioned on both product content and exemplars, and iteratively refines outputs via evaluator-generator loops that enforce relevance, promotional strength, and compliance. On both proprietary e-commerce data and the Amazon Reviews corpus, MetaSynth outperforms strong baselines across NDCG, MRR, and rank metrics. Large-scale A/B tests further demonstrate 10.26% CTR and 7.51% clicks. Beyond metadata, this work contributes a general paradigm for optimizing content in black-box systems using implicit signals."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"IEEE Big Data 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01523","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:51:17Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:51:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-05T03:06:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01617","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01617","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01617","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui Yi","familyName":"Leong","name":"Leong, Hui Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqing","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenwen","familyName":"Ouyang","name":"Ouyang, Wenwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiechao","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jiechao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AMAS: Adaptively Determining Communication Topology for LLM-based Multi-Agent System"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T02:50:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:21:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-06T09:33:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:22:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T03:16:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:23:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although large language models (LLMs) have revolutionized natural language processing capabilities, their practical implementation as autonomous multi-agent systems (MAS) for industrial problem-solving encounters persistent barriers. Conventional MAS architectures are fundamentally restricted by inflexible, hand-crafted graph topologies that lack contextual responsiveness, resulting in diminished efficacy across varied academic and commercial workloads. To surmount these constraints, we introduce AMAS, a paradigm-shifting framework that redefines LLM-based MAS through a novel dynamic graph designer. This component autonomously identifies task-specific optimal graph configurations via lightweight LLM adaptation, eliminating the reliance on monolithic, universally applied structural templates. Instead, AMAS exploits the intrinsic properties of individual inputs to intelligently direct query trajectories through task-optimized agent pathways. Rigorous validation across question answering, mathematical deduction, and code generation benchmarks confirms that AMAS systematically exceeds state-of-the-art single-agent and multi-agent approaches across diverse LLM architectures. Our investigation establishes that context-sensitive structural adaptability constitutes a foundational requirement for high-performance LLM MAS deployments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by EMNLP-2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01617","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:53:35Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:53:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-30T02:18:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01600","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01600","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01600","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neal Gregory","familyName":"Lawton","name":"Lawton, Neal Gregory","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alfy","familyName":"Samuel","name":"Samuel, Alfy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anoop","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Anoop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daben","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Daben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Comparison of Independent and Joint Fine-tuning Strategies for Retrieval-Augmented Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T02:30:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:20:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-17T20:14:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:09:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A Comparison of Independent and Joint Fine-tuning Strategies for Retrieval-Augmented Generation Download PDF Neal Gregory Lawton, Alfy Samuel, Anoop Kumar, Daben Liu Published: 20 Aug 2025, Retrieval augmented generation (RAG) is a popular framework for question answering that is powered by two large language models (LLMs): an embedding model that retrieves context documents from a database that are relevant to a given question, and a generator model that uses the retrieved context to generate an answer to the question. Both the embedding and generator models can be fine-tuned to increase performance of a RAG pipeline on a new task, but multiple fine-tuning strategies exist with different costs and benefits. In this paper, we evaluate and compare several RAG fine-tuning strategies, including independent, joint, and two-phase fine-tuning. In our experiments, we observe that all of these strategies achieve about equal improvement in EM and F1 generation quality metrics, although they have significantly different computational costs. We conclude the optimal fine-tuning strategy to use depends on whether the training dataset includes context labels and whether a grid search over the learning rates for the embedding and generator models is required."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01600","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:53:10Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:53:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:42:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02271","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02271","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02271","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaxin","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanshuo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuanshuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiyuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gongyi","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Gongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianghe","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Xianghe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Menglan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Menglan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feiyu","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Feiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Siheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"InfoMosaic-Bench: Evaluating Multi-Source Information Seeking in Tool-Augmented Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:48:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:02:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-04T09:18:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:29:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Information seeking is a fundamental requirement for humans. However, existing LLM agents rely heavily on open-web search, which exposes two fundamental weaknesses: online content is noisy and unreliable, and many real-world tasks require precise, domain-specific knowledge unavailable from the web. The emergence of the Model Context Protocol (MCP) now allows agents to interface with thousands of specialized tools, seemingly resolving this limitation. Yet it remains unclear whether agents can effectively leverage such tools -- and more importantly, whether they can integrate them with general-purpose search to solve complex tasks. Therefore, we introduce InfoMosaic-Bench, the first benchmark dedicated to multi-source information seeking in tool-augmented agents. Covering six representative domains (medicine, finance, maps, video, web, and multi-domain integration), InfoMosaic-Bench requires agents to combine general-purpose search with domain-specific tools. Tasks are synthesized with InfoMosaic-Flow, a scalable pipeline that grounds task conditions in verified tool outputs, enforces cross-source dependencies, and filters out shortcut cases solvable by trivial lookup. This design guarantees both reliability and non-triviality. Experiments with 14 state-of-the-art LLM agents reveal three findings: (i) web information alone is insufficient, with GPT-5 achieving only 38.2% accuracy and 67.5% pass rate; (ii) domain tools provide selective but inconsistent benefits, improving some domains while degrading others; and (iii) 22.4% of failures arise from incorrect tool usage or selection, highlighting that current LLMs still struggle with even basic tool handling."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02271","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:09:56Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:09:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:55:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01932","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01932","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01932","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"He","name":"He, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiusi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiusi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingxiang","familyName":"He","name":"He, Bingxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi R.","familyName":"Fung","name":"Fung, Yi R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Veri-R1: Toward Precise and Faithful Claim Verification via Online Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T11:49:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:41:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-04T07:24:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:26:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Claim verification with large language models (LLMs) has recently attracted growing attention, due to their strong reasoning capabilities and transparent verification processes compared to traditional answer-only judgments. However, existing approaches to online claim verification, which requires iterative evidence retrieval and reasoning, still mainly rely on prompt engineering or pre-designed reasoning workflows, without unified training to improve necessary skills. Therefore, we introduce Veri-R1, an online reinforcement learning (RL) framework that enables an LLM to interact with a search engine and to receive reward signals that explicitly shape its planning, retrieval, and reasoning behaviors. This dynamic interaction of LLM with retrieval systems more accurately reflects real-world verification scenarios and fosters comprehensive verification skills. Empirical results show that Veri-R1 improves joint accuracy by up to 30% and doubles the evidence score, often surpassing its larger-scale model counterparts. Ablation studies further reveal the impact of reward components, and the link between output logits and label accuracy. Our results highlight the effectiveness of online RL for precise and faithful claim verification, providing an important foundation for future research. We release our code to support community progress in LLM empowered claim verification."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01932","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:01:33Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:01:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:55:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02084","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02084","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02084","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuiye","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Kuiye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fanda","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Fanda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheya","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zheya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongxiao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hongxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunjie","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Chunjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianfeng","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Jianfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"KAIROS: Unified Training for Universal Non-Autoregressive Time Series Forecasting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T14:50:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:51:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T05:10:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-06T00:21:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In the World Wide Web, reliable time series forecasts provide the forward-looking signals that drive resource planning, cache placement, and anomaly response, enabling platforms to operate efficiently as user behavior and content distributions evolve. Compared with other domains, time series forecasting for Web applications requires much faster responsiveness to support real-time decision making. We present KAIROS, a non-autoregressive time series forecasting framework that directly models segment-level multi-peak distributions. Unlike autoregressive approaches, KAIROS avoids error accumulation and achieves just-in-time inference, while improving over existing non-autoregressive models that collapse to over-smoothed predictions. Trained on the large-scale corpus, KAIROS demonstrates strong zero-shot generalization on six widely used benchmarks, delivering forecasting performance comparable to state-of-the-art foundation models with similar scale, at a fraction of their inference cost. Beyond empirical results, KAIROS highlights the importance of non-autoregressive design as a scalable paradigm for foundation models in time series."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02084","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:05:19Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:05:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-06T02:01:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01620","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01620","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01620","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peidong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Peidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjiang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Junjiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaowen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shaowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiqing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuhao","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Xuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyi","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Siyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning to Decide with Just Enough: Information-Theoretic Context Summarization for CMDPs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T02:52:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:21:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T02:17:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-06T00:17:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Contextual Markov Decision Processes (CMDPs) offer a framework for sequential decision-making under external signals, but existing methods often fail to generalize in high-dimensional or unstructured contexts, resulting in excessive computation and unstable performance. We propose an information-theoretic summarization approach that uses large language models (LLMs) to compress contextual inputs into low-dimensional, semantically rich summaries. These summaries augment states by preserving decision-critical cues while reducing redundancy. Building on the notion of approximate context sufficiency, we provide, to our knowledge, the first regret bounds and a latency-entropy trade-off characterization for CMDPs. Our analysis clarifies how informativeness impacts computational cost. Experiments across discrete, continuous, visual, and recommendation benchmarks show that our method outperforms raw-context and non-context baselines, improving reward, success rate, and sample efficiency, while reducing latency and memory usage. These findings demonstrate that LLM-based summarization offers a scalable and interpretable solution for efficient decision-making in context-rich, resource-constrained environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01620","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:53:40Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:53:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-06T02:01:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02241","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02241","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02241","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Gwon","name":"Gwon, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nour","familyName":"Jedidi","name":"Jedidi, Nour","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jimmy","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Jimmy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Study on LLMs for Promptagator-Style Dense Retriever Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:29:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:01:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Promptagator demonstrated that Large Language Models (LLMs) with few-shot prompts can be used as task-specific query generators for fine-tuning domain-specialized dense retrieval models. However, the original Promptagator approach relied on proprietary and large-scale LLMs which users may not have access to or may be prohibited from using with sensitive data. In this work, we study the impact of open-source LLMs at accessible scales ($\\leq$14B parameters) as an alternative. Our results demonstrate that open-source LLMs as small as 3B parameters can serve as effective Promptagator-style query generators. We hope our work will inform practitioners with reliable alternatives for synthetic data generation and give insights to maximize fine-tuning results for domain-specific applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CIKM 2025 short research paper"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02241","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:09:12Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:09:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:09:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02232","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02232","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02232","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ebtesam Jaber","familyName":"Aljohani","name":"Aljohani, Ebtesam Jaber","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wael M. S.","familyName":"Yafoo","name":"Yafoo, Wael M. S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhanced Arabic-language cyberbullying detection: deep embedding and transformer (BERT) approaches"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:20:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:00:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.11591/ijai.v14.i3.pp2258-2269","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent technological advances in smartphones and communications, including the growth of such online platforms as massive social media networks such as X (formerly known as Twitter) endangers young people and their emotional well-being by exposing them to cyberbullying, taunting, and bullying content. Most proposed approaches for automatically detecting cyberbullying have been developed around the English language, and methods for detecting Arabic-language cyberbullying are scarce. Methods for detecting Arabic-language cyberbullying are especially scarce. This paper aims to enhance the effectiveness of methods for detecting cyberbullying in Arabic-language content. We assembled a dataset of 10,662 X posts, pre-processed the data, and used the kappa tool to verify and enhance the quality of our annotations. We conducted four experiments to test numerous deep learning models for automatically detecting Arabic-language cyberbullying. We first tested a long short-term memory (LSTM) model and a bidirectional long short-term memory (Bi-LSTM) model with several experimental word embeddings. We also tested the LSTM and Bi-LSTM models with a novel pre-trained bidirectional encoder from representations (BERT) and then tested them on a different experimental models BERT again. LSTM-BERT and Bi-LSTM-BERT demonstrated a 97% accuracy. Bi-LSTM with FastText embedding word performed even better, achieving 98% accuracy. As a result, the outcomes are generalize"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02232","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-03T02:08:58Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:08:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:08:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02204","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02204","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02204","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingzhong","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Lingzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ziqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuaibo","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Shuaibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiyue","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Haiyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengzhou","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Pengzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongru","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zongru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gongshen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Gongshen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuosheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhuosheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Say One Thing, Do Another? Diagnosing Reasoning-Execution Gaps in VLM-Powered Mobile-Use Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T16:51:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:59:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mobile-use agents powered by vision-language models (VLMs) have shown great potential in interpreting natural language instructions and generating corresponding actions based on mobile graphical user interface. Recent studies suggest that incorporating chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning tends to improve the execution accuracy. However, existing evaluations emphasize execution accuracy while neglecting whether CoT reasoning aligns with ground-truth actions. This oversight fails to assess potential reasoning-execution gaps, which in turn foster over-trust: users relying on seemingly plausible CoTs may unknowingly authorize harmful actions, potentially resulting in financial loss or trust crisis. In this work, we introduce a new evaluation framework to diagnose reasoning-execution gaps. At its core lies Ground-Truth Alignment (GTA), which measures whether the action implied by a CoT matches the ground-truth action. By combining GTA with the standard Exact Match (EM) metric, we jointly assess both the reasoning accuracy and execution accuracy. This joint perspective reveals two types of reasoning-execution gaps: (i) Execution Gap (EG), where the reasoning correctly identifies the correct action but execution fails, and (ii) Reasoning Gap (RG), where execution succeeds but reasoning process conflicts with the actual execution. Experimental results across a wide range of mobile interaction tasks reveal that reasoning-execution gaps are prevalent, with execution gaps occurring more frequently than reasoning gaps. Moreover, while scaling up model size reduces the overall gap, sizable execution gaps persist even in the largest models. Further analysis shows that our framework reliably reflects systematic EG/RG patterns in state-of-the-art models. These findings offer concrete diagnostics and support the development of more trustworthy mobile-use agents."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02204","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:08:17Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:08:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:08:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02200","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02200","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02200","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felix","familyName":"Brei","name":"Brei, Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenz","familyName":"Bühmann","name":"Bühmann, Lorenz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johannes","familyName":"Frey","name":"Frey, Johannes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Gerber","name":"Gerber, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lars-Peter","familyName":"Meyer","name":"Meyer, Lars-Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claus","familyName":"Stadler","name":"Stadler, Claus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kirill","familyName":"Bulert","name":"Bulert, Kirill","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ARUQULA -- An LLM based Text2SPARQL Approach using ReAct and Knowledge Graph Exploration Utilities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T16:49:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:59:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Interacting with knowledge graphs can be a daunting task for people without a background in computer science since the query language that is used (SPARQL) has a high barrier of entry. Large language models (LLMs) can lower that barrier by providing support in the form of Text2SPARQL translation. In this paper we introduce a generalized method based on SPINACH, an LLM backed agent that translates natural language questions to SPARQL queries not in a single shot, but as an iterative process of exploration and execution. We describe the overall architecture and reasoning behind our design decisions, and also conduct a thorough analysis of the agent behavior to gain insights into future areas for targeted improvements. This work was motivated by the Text2SPARQL challenge, a challenge that was held to facilitate improvements in the Text2SPARQL domain."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"peer reviewed publication at Text2SPARQL Workshop @ ESWC 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02200","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:08:11Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:08:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:08:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02185","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02185","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02185","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paschal C.","familyName":"Amusuo","name":"Amusuo, Paschal C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongge","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dongge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ricardo Andres Calvo","familyName":"Mendez","name":"Mendez, Ricardo Andres Calvo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Metzman","name":"Metzman, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oliver","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Oliver","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James C.","familyName":"Davis","name":"Davis, James C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FalseCrashReducer: Mitigating False Positive Crashes in OSS-Fuzz-Gen Using Agentic AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"D.2.4; F.3.1","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T16:36:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:58:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fuzz testing has become a cornerstone technique for identifying software bugs and security vulnerabilities, with broad adoption in both industry and open-source communities. Directly fuzzing a function requires fuzz drivers, which translate random fuzzer inputs into valid arguments for the target function. Given the cost and expertise required to manually develop fuzz drivers, methods exist that leverage program analysis and Large Language Models to automatically generate these drivers. However, the generated fuzz drivers frequently lead to false positive crashes, especially in functions highly structured input and complex state requirements. This problem is especially crucial in industry-scale fuzz driver generation efforts like OSS-Fuzz-en, as reporting false positive crashes to maintainers impede trust in both the system and the team.\n This paper presents two AI-driven strategies to reduce false positives in OSS-Fuzz-Gen, a multi-agent system for automated fuzz driver generation. First, constraint-based fuzz driver generation proactively enforces constraints on a function's inputs and state to guide driver creation. Second, context-based crash validation reactively analyzes function callers to determine whether reported crashes are feasible from program entry points. Using 1,500 benchmark functions from OSS-Fuzz, we show that these strategies reduce spurious crashes by up to 8%, cut reported crashes by more than half, and demonstrate that frontier LLMs can serve as reliable program analysis agents. Our results highlight the promise and challenges of integrating AI into large-scale fuzzing pipelines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02185","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:07:48Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:07:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:07:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02139","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02139","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02139","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florensia","familyName":"Widjaja","name":"Widjaja, Florensia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhangtianyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhangtianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juexiao","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Juexiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BioinfoMCP: A Unified Platform Enabling MCP Interfaces in Agentic Bioinformatics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T15:47:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:55:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Bioinformatics tools are essential for complex computational biology tasks, yet their integration with emerging AI-agent frameworks is hindered by incompatible interfaces, heterogeneous input-output formats, and inconsistent parameter conventions. The Model Context Protocol (MCP) provides a standardized framework for tool-AI communication, but manually converting hundreds of existing and rapidly growing specialized bioinformatics tools into MCP-compliant servers is labor-intensive and unsustainable. Here, we present BioinfoMCP, a unified platform comprising two components: BioinfoMCP Converter, which automatically generates robust MCP servers from tool documentation using large language models, and BioinfoMCP Benchmark, which systematically validates the reliability and versatility of converted tools across diverse computational tasks. We present a platform of 38 MCP-converted bioinformatics tools, extensively validated to show that 94.7% successfully executed complex workflows across three widely used AI-agent platforms. By removing technical barriers to AI automation, BioinfoMCP enables natural-language interaction with sophisticated bioinformatics analyses without requiring extensive programming expertise, offering a scalable path to intelligent, interoperable computational biology."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages, 8 figures, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02139","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:06:40Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:06:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:06:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02044","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02044","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02044","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siddhant","familyName":"Arora","name":"Arora, Siddhant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haidar","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Haidar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin Luna","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Xin Luna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sajal","familyName":"Choudhary","name":"Choudhary, Sajal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seungwhan","familyName":"Moon","name":"Moon, Seungwhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adithya","familyName":"Sagar","name":"Sagar, Adithya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Surya Teja","familyName":"Appini","name":"Appini, Surya Teja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaushik","familyName":"Patnaik","name":"Patnaik, Kaushik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanat","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Sanat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shinji","familyName":"Watanabe","name":"Watanabe, Shinji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anuj","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Anuj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"Aly","name":"Aly, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florian","familyName":"Metze","name":"Metze, Florian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaojiang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zhaojiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stream RAG: Instant and Accurate Spoken Dialogue Systems with Streaming Tool Usage"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T14:18:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:49:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"End-to-end speech-in speech-out dialogue systems are emerging as a powerful alternative to traditional ASR-LLM-TTS pipelines, generating more natural, expressive responses with significantly lower latency. However, these systems remain prone to hallucinations due to limited factual grounding. While text-based dialogue systems address this challenge by integrating tools such as web search and knowledge graph APIs, we introduce the first approach to extend tool use directly into speech-in speech-out systems. A key challenge is that tool integration substantially increases response latency, disrupting conversational flow. To mitigate this, we propose Streaming Retrieval-Augmented Generation (Streaming RAG), a novel framework that reduces user-perceived latency by predicting tool queries in parallel with user speech, even before the user finishes speaking. Specifically, we develop a post-training pipeline that teaches the model when to issue tool calls during ongoing speech and how to generate spoken summaries that fuse audio queries with retrieved text results, thereby improving both accuracy and responsiveness. To evaluate our approach, we construct AudioCRAG, a benchmark created by converting queries from the publicly available CRAG dataset into speech form. Experimental results demonstrate that our streaming RAG approach increases QA accuracy by up to 200% relative (from 11.1% to 34.2% absolute) and further enhances user experience by reducing tool use latency by 20%. Importantly, our streaming RAG approach is modality-agnostic and can be applied equally to typed input, paving the way for more agentic, real-time AI assistants."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02044","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:04:19Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:04:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:04:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01995","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01995","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01995","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Arid","familyName":"Hasan","name":"Hasan, Md Arid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Firoj","familyName":"Alam","name":"Alam, Firoj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Fahad","familyName":"Hossain","name":"Hossain, Md Fahad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Usman","familyName":"Naseem","name":"Naseem, Usman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Syed Ishtiaque","familyName":"Ahmed","name":"Ahmed, Syed Ishtiaque","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Based Multi-Task Bangla Hate Speech Detection: Type, Severity, and Target"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"F.2.2; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T50","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T13:17:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:45:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Online social media platforms are central to everyday communication and information seeking. While these platforms serve positive purposes, they also provide fertile ground for the spread of hate speech, offensive language, and bullying content targeting individuals, organizations, and communities. Such content undermines safety, participation, and equity online. Reliable detection systems are therefore needed, especially for low-resource languages where moderation tools are limited. In Bangla, prior work has contributed resources and models, but most are single-task (e.g., binary hate/offense) with limited coverage of multi-facet signals (type, severity, target). We address these gaps by introducing the first multi-task Bangla hate-speech dataset, BanglaMultiHate, one of the largest manually annotated corpus to date. Building on this resource, we conduct a comprehensive, controlled comparison spanning classical baselines, monolingual pretrained models, and LLMs under zero-shot prompting and LoRA fine-tuning. Our experiments assess LLM adaptability in a low-resource setting and reveal a consistent trend: although LoRA-tuned LLMs are competitive with BanglaBERT, culturally and linguistically grounded pretraining remains critical for robust performance. Together, our dataset and findings establish a stronger benchmark for developing culturally aligned moderation tools in low-resource contexts. For reproducibility, we will release the dataset and all related scripts."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01995","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:03:05Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:03:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:03:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01989","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01989","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01989","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryosuke","familyName":"Kohita","name":"Kohita, Ryosuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring Database Normalization Effects on SQL Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T13:11:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:45:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Schema design, particularly normalization, is a critical yet often overlooked factor in natural language to SQL (NL2SQL) systems. Most prior research evaluates models on fixed schemas, overlooking the influence of design on performance. We present the first systematic study of schema normalization's impact, evaluating eight leading large language models on synthetic and real-world datasets with varied normalization levels. We construct controlled synthetic datasets with formal normalization (1NF-3NF) and real academic paper datasets with practical schemes. Our results show that denormalized schemas offer high accuracy on simple retrieval queries, even with cost-effective models in zero-shot settings. In contrast, normalized schemas (2NF/3NF) introduce challenges such as errors in base table selection and join type prediction; however, these issues are substantially mitigated by providing few-shot examples. For aggregation queries, normalized schemas yielded better performance, mainly due to their robustness against the data duplication and NULL value issues that cause errors in denormalized schemas. These findings suggest that the optimal schema design for NL2SQL applications depends on the types of queries to be supported. Our study demonstrates the importance of considering schema design when developing NL2SQL interfaces and integrating adaptive schema selection for real-world scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to CIKM 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01989","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:02:56Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:02:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:02:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01910","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01910","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01910","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoyan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhaoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arogya","familyName":"Kharel","name":"Kharel, Arogya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"In-Young","familyName":"Ko","name":"Ko, In-Young","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are LLMs Better GNN Helpers? Rethinking Robust Graph Learning under Deficiencies with Iterative Refinement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T11:30:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:40:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) are widely adopted in Web-related applications, serving as a core technique for learning from graph-structured data, such as text-attributed graphs. Yet in real-world scenarios, such graphs exhibit deficiencies that substantially undermine GNN performance. While prior GNN-based augmentation studies have explored robustness against individual imperfections, a systematic understanding of how graph-native and Large Language Models (LLMs) enhanced methods behave under compound deficiencies is still missing. Specifically, there has been no comprehensive investigation comparing conventional approaches and recent LLM-on-graph frameworks, leaving their merits unclear. To fill this gap, we conduct the first empirical study that benchmarks these two lines of methods across diverse graph deficiencies, revealing overlooked vulnerabilities and challenging the assumption that LLM augmentation is consistently superior. Building on empirical findings, we propose Robust Graph Learning via Retrieval-Augmented Contrastive Refinement (RoGRAD) framework. Unlike prior one-shot LLM-as-Enhancer designs, RoGRAD is the first iterative paradigm that leverages Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to inject retrieval-grounded augmentations by supplying class-consistent, diverse augmentations and enforcing discriminative representations through iterative graph contrastive learning. It transforms LLM augmentation for graphs from static signal injection into dynamic refinement. Extensive experiments demonstrate RoGRAD's superiority over both conventional GNN- and LLM-enhanced baselines, achieving up to 82.43% average improvement."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01910","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:00:59Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:01:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:01:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01842","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01842","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01842","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yannis","familyName":"Belkhiter","name":"Belkhiter, Yannis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seshu","familyName":"Tirupathi","name":"Tirupathi, Seshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giulio","familyName":"Zizzo","name":"Zizzo, Giulio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sachin","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Sachin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John D.","familyName":"Kelleher","name":"Kelleher, John D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pre-Hoc Predictions in AutoML: Leveraging LLMs to Enhance Model Selection and Benchmarking for Tabular datasets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T09:37:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:36:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The field of AutoML has made remarkable progress in post-hoc model selection, with libraries capable of automatically identifying the most performing models for a given dataset. Nevertheless, these methods often rely on exhaustive hyperparameter searches, where methods automatically train and test different types of models on the target dataset. Contrastingly, pre-hoc prediction emerges as a promising alternative, capable of bypassing exhaustive search through intelligent pre-selection of models. Despite its potential, pre-hoc prediction remains under-explored in the literature. This paper explores the intersection of AutoML and pre-hoc model selection by leveraging traditional models and Large Language Model (LLM) agents to reduce the search space of AutoML libraries. By relying on dataset descriptions and statistical information, we reduce the AutoML search space. Our methodology is applied to the AWS AutoGluon portfolio dataset, a state-of-the-art AutoML benchmark containing 175 tabular classification datasets available on OpenML. The proposed approach offers a shift in AutoML workflows, significantly reducing computational overhead, while still selecting the best model for the given dataset."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Oral Presentations ADAPT Annual Scientific Conference 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01842","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:59:17Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:59:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:59:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01817","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01817","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01817","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Filipek","name":"Filipek, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sparse Query Attention (SQA): A Computationally Efficient Attention Mechanism with Query Heads Reduction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T09:01:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:34:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Transformer architecture, underpinned by the Multi-Head Attention (MHA) mechanism, has become the de facto standard for state-of-the-art models in artificial intelligence. However, the quadratic computational complexity of MHA with respect to sequence length presents a significant barrier to scaling, particularly for applications involving long contexts. Prevailing solutions, such as Multi-Query Attention (MQA) and Grouped-Query Attention (GQA), have effectively addressed the memory bandwidth bottleneck that dominates autoregressive inference latency by sharing Key and Value projections. While highly successful, these methods do not reduce the fundamental number of floating-point operations (FLOPs) required for the attention score computation, which remains a critical bottleneck for training and full-sequence processing. This paper introduces Sparse Query Attention (SQA), a novel attention architecture that pursues an alternative and complementary optimization path. Instead of reducing Key/Value heads, SQA reduces the number of Query heads. This architectural modification directly decreases the computational complexity of the attention mechanism by a factor proportional to the reduction in query heads, thereby lowering the overall FLOPs. This work presents the theoretical foundation of SQA, its mathematical formulation, and a family of architectural variants. Empirical benchmarks on long sequences (32k-200k tokens) demonstrate that SQA can achieve significant throughput improvements of up to 3x in computation-bound scenarios such as model pre-training, fine-tuning, and encoder-based tasks, with only a minimal impact on model quality in preliminary smallscale experiments. SQA was discovered serendipitously during the development of the upcoming Reactive Transformer architecture, suggesting its potential as a powerful tool for building more efficient and scalable models"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 6 figures, small-scale experiments"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01817","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:58:39Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:58:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:58:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01792","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01792","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01792","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivan Leonidovich","familyName":"Litvak","name":"Litvak, Ivan Leonidovich","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anton","familyName":"Kostin","name":"Kostin, Anton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fedor","familyName":"Lashkin","name":"Lashkin, Fedor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatiana","familyName":"Maksiyan","name":"Maksiyan, Tatiana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergey","familyName":"Lagutin","name":"Lagutin, Sergey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Comparison of Unsupervised Metrics for Evaluating Judicial Decision Extraction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"H.3.3; I.2.8; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T08:32:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:33:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid advancement of artificial intelligence in legal natural language processing demands scalable methods for evaluating text extraction from judicial decisions. This study evaluates 16 unsupervised metrics, including novel formulations, to assess the quality of extracting seven semantic blocks from 1,000 anonymized Russian judicial decisions, validated against 7,168 expert reviews on a 1--5 Likert scale. These metrics, spanning document-based, semantic, structural, pseudo-ground truth, and legal-specific categories, operate without pre-annotated ground truth. Bootstrapped correlations, Lin's concordance correlation coefficient (CCC), and mean absolute error (MAE) reveal that Term Frequency Coherence (Pearson $r = 0.540$, Lin CCC = 0.512, MAE = 0.127) and Coverage Ratio/Block Completeness (Pearson $r = 0.513$, Lin CCC = 0.443, MAE = 0.139) best align with expert ratings, while Legal Term Density (Pearson $r = -0.479$, Lin CCC = -0.079, MAE = 0.394) show strong negative correlations. The LLM Evaluation Score (mean = 0.849, Pearson $r = 0.382$, Lin CCC = 0.325, MAE = 0.197) showed moderate alignment, but its performance, using gpt-4.1-mini via g4f, suggests limited specialization for legal textse. These findings highlight that unsupervised metrics, including LLM-based approaches, enable scalable screening but, with moderate correlations and low CCC values, cannot fully replace human judgment in high-stakes legal contexts. This work advances legal NLP by providing annotation-free evaluation tools, with implications for judicial analytics and ethical AI deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01792","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:58:02Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:58:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:58:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01681","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01681","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01681","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuchen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuzhao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xuzhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahui","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jiahui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renjie","familyName":"Pi","name":"Pi, Renjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyu","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Shiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wentao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Look Less, Reason More: Rollout-Guided Adaptive Pixel-Space Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T05:14:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:25:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) excel at many multimodal tasks, yet they frequently struggle with tasks requiring precise understanding and handling of fine-grained visual elements. This is mainly due to information loss during image encoding or insufficient attention to critical regions. Recent work has shown promise by incorporating pixel-level visual information into the reasoning process, enabling VLMs to access high-resolution visual details during their thought process. However, this pixel-level information is often overused, leading to inefficiency and distraction from irrelevant visual details. To address these challenges, we propose the first framework for adaptive pixel reasoning that dynamically determines necessary pixel-level operations based on the input query. Specifically, we first apply operation-aware supervised fine-tuning to establish baseline competence in textual reasoning and visual operations, then design a novel rollout-guided reinforcement learning framework relying on feedback of the model's own responses, which enables the VLM to determine when pixel operations should be invoked based on query difficulty. Experiments on extensive multimodal reasoning benchmarks show that our model achieves superior performance while significantly reducing unnecessary visual operations. Impressively, our model achieves 73.4\\% accuracy on HR-Bench 4K while maintaining a tool usage ratio of only 20.1\\%, improving accuracy and simultaneously reducing tool usage by 66.5\\% compared to the previous methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint, Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01681","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:55:14Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:55:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:55:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01670","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01670","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01670","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erfan","familyName":"Shayegani","name":"Shayegani, Erfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keegan","familyName":"Hines","name":"Hines, Keegan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nael","familyName":"Abu-Ghazaleh","name":"Abu-Ghazaleh, Nael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roman","familyName":"Lutz","name":"Lutz, Roman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Spencer","familyName":"Whitehead","name":"Whitehead, Spencer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vidhisha","familyName":"Balachandran","name":"Balachandran, Vidhisha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Besmira","familyName":"Nushi","name":"Nushi, Besmira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vibhav","familyName":"Vineet","name":"Vineet, Vibhav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Just Do It!? Computer-Use Agents Exhibit Blind Goal-Directedness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T04:52:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:25:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Computer-Use Agents (CUAs) are an increasingly deployed class of agents that take actions on GUIs to accomplish user goals. In this paper, we show that CUAs consistently exhibit Blind Goal-Directedness (BGD): a bias to pursue goals regardless of feasibility, safety, reliability, or context. We characterize three prevalent patterns of BGD: (i) lack of contextual reasoning, (ii) assumptions and decisions under ambiguity, and (iii) contradictory or infeasible goals. We develop BLIND-ACT, a benchmark of 90 tasks capturing these three patterns. Built on OSWorld, BLIND-ACT provides realistic environments and employs LLM-based judges to evaluate agent behavior, achieving 93.75% agreement with human annotations. We use BLIND-ACT to evaluate nine frontier models, including Claude Sonnet and Opus 4, Computer-Use-Preview, and GPT-5, observing high average BGD rates (80.8%) across them. We show that BGD exposes subtle risks that arise even when inputs are not directly harmful. While prompting-based interventions lower BGD levels, substantial risk persists, highlighting the need for stronger training- or inference-time interventions. Qualitative analysis reveals observed failure modes: execution-first bias (focusing on how to act over whether to act), thought-action disconnect (execution diverging from reasoning), and request-primacy (justifying actions due to user request). Identifying BGD and introducing BLIND-ACT establishes a foundation for future research on studying and mitigating this fundamental risk and ensuring safe CUA deployment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01670","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:54:58Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:54:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:54:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01664","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01664","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01664","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yejin","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Yejin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youngbin","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Youngbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juhyeong","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Juhyeong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongjae","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Yongjae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GuruAgents: Emulating Wise Investors with Prompt-Guided LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T04:45:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:25:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study demonstrates that GuruAgents, prompt-guided AI agents, can systematically operationalize the strategies of legendary investment gurus. We develop five distinct GuruAgents, each designed to emulate an iconic investor, by encoding their distinct philosophies into LLM prompts that integrate financial tools and a deterministic reasoning pipeline. In a backtest on NASDAQ-100 constituents from Q4 2023 to Q2 2025, the GuruAgents exhibit unique behaviors driven by their prompted personas. The Buffett GuruAgent achieves the highest performance, delivering a 42.2\\% CAGR that significantly outperforms benchmarks, while other agents show varied results. These findings confirm that prompt engineering can successfully translate the qualitative philosophies of investment gurus into reproducible, quantitative strategies, highlighting a novel direction for automated systematic investing. The source code and data are available at https://github.com/yejining99/GuruAgents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 Pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01664","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:54:49Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:54:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:54:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01645","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01645","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01645","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niloofar","familyName":"Mireshghallah","name":"Mireshghallah, Niloofar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Position: Privacy Is Not Just Memorization!"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T04:02:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:23:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The discourse on privacy risks in Large Language Models (LLMs) has disproportionately focused on verbatim memorization of training data, while a constellation of more immediate and scalable privacy threats remain underexplored. This position paper argues that the privacy landscape of LLM systems extends far beyond training data extraction, encompassing risks from data collection practices, inference-time context leakage, autonomous agent capabilities, and the democratization of surveillance through deep inference attacks. We present a comprehensive taxonomy of privacy risks across the LLM lifecycle -- from data collection through deployment -- and demonstrate through case studies how current privacy frameworks fail to address these multifaceted threats. Through a longitudinal analysis of 1,322 AI/ML privacy papers published at leading conferences over the past decade (2016--2025), we reveal that while memorization receives outsized attention in technical research, the most pressing privacy harms lie elsewhere, where current technical approaches offer little traction and viable paths forward remain unclear. We call for a fundamental shift in how the research community approaches LLM privacy, moving beyond the narrow focus of current technical solutions and embracing interdisciplinary approaches that address the sociotechnical nature of these emerging threats."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 6 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01645","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:54:16Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:54:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:54:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01639","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01639","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01639","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thinh Hung","familyName":"Truong","name":"Truong, Thinh Hung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jey Han","familyName":"Lau","name":"Lau, Jey Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianzhong","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Jianzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Understanding the Geospatial Reasoning Capabilities of LLMs: A Trajectory Recovery Perspective"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T03:37:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:23:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We explore the geospatial reasoning capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs), specifically, whether LLMs can read road network maps and perform navigation. We frame trajectory recovery as a proxy task, which requires models to reconstruct masked GPS traces, and introduce GLOBALTRACE, a dataset with over 4,000 real-world trajectories across diverse regions and transportation modes. Using road network as context, our prompting framework enables LLMs to generate valid paths without accessing any external navigation tools. Experiments show that LLMs outperform off-the-shelf baselines and specialized trajectory recovery models, with strong zero-shot generalization. Fine-grained analysis shows that LLMs have strong comprehension of the road network and coordinate systems, but also pose systematic biases with respect to regions and transportation modes. Finally, we demonstrate how LLMs can enhance navigation experiences by reasoning over maps in flexible ways to incorporate user preferences."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01639","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:54:07Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:54:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:54:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01638","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01638","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01638","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siying","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Siying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaxing","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Yaxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhicong","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Zhicong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Human-Centered RegTech: Unpacking Professionals' Strategies and Needs for Using LLMs Safely"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T03:35:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:23:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models are profoundly changing work patterns in high-risk professional domains, yet their application also introduces severe and underexplored compliance risks. To investigate this issue, we conducted semi-structured interviews with 24 highly-skilled knowledge workers from industries such as law, healthcare, and finance. The study found that these experts are commonly concerned about sensitive information leakage, intellectual property infringement, and uncertainty regarding the quality of model outputs. In response, they spontaneously adopt various mitigation strategies, such as actively distorting input data and limiting the details in their prompts. However, the effectiveness of these spontaneous efforts is limited due to a lack of specific compliance guidance and training for Large Language Models. Our research reveals a significant gap between current NLP tools and the actual compliance needs of experts. This paper positions these valuable empirical findings as foundational work for building the next generation of Human-Centered, Compliance-Driven Natural Language Processing for Regulatory Technology (RegTech), providing a critical human-centered perspective and design requirements for engineering NLP systems that can proactively support expert compliance workflows."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the 4th HCI+NLP@EMNLP 2025 Workshop. (Non-archival)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01638","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:54:06Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:54:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:54:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01624","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01624","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01624","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feiyang","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Feiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Kuchnik","name":"Kuchnik, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karthik","familyName":"Padthe","name":"Padthe, Karthik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marin","familyName":"Vlastelica","name":"Vlastelica, Marin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoxi","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Ruoxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carole-Jean","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Carole-Jean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Newsha","familyName":"Ardalani","name":"Ardalani, Newsha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quagmires in SFT-RL Post-Training: When High SFT Scores Mislead and What to Use Instead"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T02:57:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:21:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In post-training for reasoning Large Language Models (LLMs), the current state of practice trains LLMs in two independent stages: Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) and Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR, shortened as ``RL'' below). In this work, we challenge whether high SFT scores translate to improved performance after RL. We provide extensive counter-examples where this is not true. We find high SFT scores can be biased toward simpler or more homogeneous data and are not reliably predictive of subsequent RL gains or scaled-up post-training effectiveness. In some cases, RL training on models with improved SFT performance could lead to substantially worse outcome compared to RL on the base model without SFT. We study alternative metrics and identify generalization loss on held-out reasoning examples and Pass@large k performance to provide strong proxies for the RL outcome. We trained hundreds of models up to 12B-parameter with SFT and RLVR via GRPO and ran extensive evaluations on 7 math benchmarks with up to 256 repetitions, spending $\u0026gt;$1M GPU hours. Experiments include models from Llama3, Mistral-Nemo, Qwen3 and multiple state-of-the-art SFT/RL datasets. Compared to directly predicting from pre-RL performance, prediction based on generalization loss and Pass@large k achieves substantial higher precision, improving $R^2$ coefficient and Spearman's rank correlation coefficient by up to 0.5 (2x). This provides strong utility for broad use cases. For example, in most experiments, we find SFT training on unique examples for a one epoch underperforms training on half examples for two epochs, either after SFT or SFT-then-RL; With the same SFT budget, training only on short examples may lead to better SFT performance, though, it often leads to worse outcome after RL compared to training on examples with varying lengths. Evaluation tool will be open-sourced."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint. Under Review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01624","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:53:46Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:53:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:53:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01622","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01622","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01622","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"ZeHua","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, ZeHua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"XiaoFan","familyName":"Gui","name":"Gui, XiaoFan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"LuYao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, LuYao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Lau","name":"Lau, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM4Rec: Large Language Models for Multimodal Generative Recommendation with Causal Debiasing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T02:53:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:21:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Contemporary generative recommendation systems face significant challenges in handling multimodal data, eliminating algorithmic biases, and providing transparent decision-making processes. This paper introduces an enhanced generative recommendation framework that addresses these limitations through five key innovations: multimodal fusion architecture, retrieval-augmented generation mechanisms, causal inference-based debiasing, explainable recommendation generation, and real-time adaptive learning capabilities. Our framework leverages advanced large language models as the backbone while incorporating specialized modules for cross-modal understanding, contextual knowledge integration, bias mitigation, explanation synthesis, and continuous model adaptation. Extensive experiments on three benchmark datasets (MovieLens-25M, Amazon-Electronics, Yelp-2023) demonstrate consistent improvements in recommendation accuracy, fairness, and diversity compared to existing approaches. The proposed framework achieves up to 2.3% improvement in NDCG@10 and 1.4% enhancement in diversity metrics while maintaining computational efficiency through optimized inference strategies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01622","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:53:43Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:53:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:53:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01609","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01609","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01609","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"ZeHua","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, ZeHua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"XiaoFan","familyName":"Gui","name":"Gui, XiaoFan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"LuYao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, LuYao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Lau","name":"Lau, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentRec: Next-Generation LLM-Powered Multi-Agent Collaborative Recommendation with Adaptive Intelligence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T02:47:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:21:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Interactive conversational recommender systems have gained significant attention for their ability to capture user preferences through natural language interactions. However, existing approaches face substantial challenges in handling dynamic user preferences, maintaining conversation coherence, and balancing multiple ranking objectives simultaneously. This paper introduces AgentRec, a next-generation LLM-powered multi-agent collaborative recommendation framework that addresses these limitations through hierarchical agent networks with adaptive intelligence. Our approach employs specialized LLM-powered agents for conversation understanding, preference modeling, context awareness, and dynamic ranking, coordinated through an adaptive weighting mechanism that learns from interaction patterns. We propose a three-tier learning strategy combining rapid response for simple queries, intelligent reasoning for complex preferences, and deep collaboration for challenging scenarios. Extensive experiments on three real-world datasets demonstrate that AgentRec achieves consistent improvements over state-of-the-art baselines, with 2.8\\% enhancement in conversation success rate, 1.9\\% improvement in recommendation accuracy (NDCG@10), and 3.2\\% better conversation efficiency while maintaining comparable computational costs through intelligent agent coordination."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01609","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:53:24Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:53:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:53:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01586","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01586","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01586","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiting","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yiting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yolo Yunlong","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yolo Yunlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeliang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zeliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haozheng","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Haozheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuwei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianshu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jianshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dennis","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Dennis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong-Yu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hong-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyang","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Haoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manling","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Manling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenliang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chenliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philip S.","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Philip S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AdvEvo-MARL: Shaping Internalized Safety through Adversarial Co-Evolution in Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T02:06:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:19:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM-based multi-agent systems excel at planning, tool use, and role coordination, but their openness and interaction complexity also expose them to jailbreak, prompt-injection, and adversarial collaboration. Existing defenses fall into two lines: (i) self-verification that asks each agent to pre-filter unsafe instructions before execution, and (ii) external guard modules that police behaviors. The former often underperforms because a standalone agent lacks sufficient capacity to detect cross-agent unsafe chains and delegation-induced risks; the latter increases system overhead and creates a single-point-of-failure-once compromised, system-wide safety collapses, and adding more guards worsens cost and complexity. To solve these challenges, we propose AdvEvo-MARL, a co-evolutionary multi-agent reinforcement learning framework that internalizes safety into task agents. Rather than relying on external guards, AdvEvo-MARL jointly optimizes attackers (which synthesize evolving jailbreak prompts) and defenders (task agents trained to both accomplish their duties and resist attacks) in adversarial learning environments. To stabilize learning and foster cooperation, we introduce a public baseline for advantage estimation: agents within the same functional group share a group-level mean-return baseline, enabling lower-variance updates and stronger intra-group coordination. Across representative attack scenarios, AdvEvo-MARL consistently keeps attack-success rate (ASR) below 20%, whereas baselines reach up to 38.33%, while preserving-and sometimes improving-task accuracy (up to +3.67% on reasoning tasks). These results show that safety and utility can be jointly improved without relying on extra guard agents or added system overhead."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01586","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:52:49Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:52:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:52:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01585","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01585","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01585","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haochen","familyName":"You","name":"You, Haochen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baojing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Baojing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ReSSFormer: A Recursive Sparse Structured Transformer for Scalable and Long-Context Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T02:05:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:19:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While Transformer architectures have demonstrated impressive scalability across domains, they continue to face challenges in long-context reasoning, computational efficiency, and structural generalization - largely due to rigid layer stacking, dense attention, and reliance on positional encodings. We present ReSSFormer, a Recursive Sparse Structured Transformer that integrates three complementary innovations: Recurrent Reasoning \u0026amp; Memory Unit (R2MU) for iterative reasoning with bounded depth, Adaptive Sparse Attention Module (ASAM) for efficient and focused context selection, and Self-Organizing Encoder Structure (SOES) for position-free structure induction. ReSSFormer replaces conventional depth stacking with recurrent inference, substitutes full attention with token- and expert-level sparsity, and models latent token topology directly from content. Across language modeling, multi-hop QA, and structure-sensitive tasks, ReSSFormer consistently outperforms strong baselines under comparable FLOPs and parameter budgets, highlighting its scalability, efficiency, and structural flexibility."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted as a short paper at ACM Multimedia Asia 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01585","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:52:48Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:52:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:52:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01531","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01531","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01531","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Djengo Cyun-Jyun","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Djengo Cyun-Jyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tsung-Wei","familyName":"Ke","name":"Ke, Tsung-Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Information Seeking for Robust Decision Making under Partial Observability"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T00:06:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:15:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Explicit information seeking is essential to human problem-solving in practical environments characterized by incomplete information and noisy dynamics. When the true environmental state is not directly observable, humans seek information to update their internal dynamics and inform future decision-making. Although existing Large Language Model (LLM) planning agents have addressed observational uncertainty, they often overlook discrepancies between their internal dynamics and the actual environment. We introduce Information Seeking Decision Planner (InfoSeeker), an LLM decision-making framework that integrates task-oriented planning with information seeking to align internal dynamics and make optimal decisions under uncertainty in both agent observations and environmental dynamics. InfoSeeker prompts an LLM to actively gather information by planning actions to validate its understanding, detect environmental changes, or test hypotheses before generating or revising task-oriented plans. To evaluate InfoSeeker, we introduce a novel benchmark suite featuring partially observable environments with incomplete observations and uncertain dynamics. Experiments demonstrate that InfoSeeker achieves a 74% absolute performance gain over prior methods without sacrificing sample efficiency. Moreover, InfoSeeker generalizes across LLMs and outperforms baselines on established benchmarks such as robotic manipulation and web navigation. These findings underscore the importance of tightly integrating planning and information seeking for robust behavior in partially observable environments. The project page is available at https://infoseekerllm.github.io"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The project page is available at https://infoseekerllm.github.io"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01531","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:51:28Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:51:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:51:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01524","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01524","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01524","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Viraj","familyName":"Prabhu","name":"Prabhu, Viraj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Fernandez","name":"Fernandez, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krithika","familyName":"Ramakrishnan","name":"Ramakrishnan, Krithika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanqi","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yanqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Silvio","familyName":"Savarese","name":"Savarese, Silvio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiming","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Caiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junnan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Junnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zeyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WALT: Web Agents that Learn Tools"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T23:41:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:14:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Web agents promise to automate complex browser tasks, but current methods remain brittle -- relying on step-by-step UI interactions and heavy LLM reasoning that break under dynamic layouts and long horizons. Humans, by contrast, exploit website-provided functionality through high-level operations like search, filter, and sort. We introduce WALT (Web Agents that Learn Tools), a framework that reverse-engineers latent website functionality into reusable invocable tools. Rather than hypothesizing ad-hoc skills, WALT exposes robust implementations of automations already designed into websites -- spanning discovery (search, filter, sort), communication (post, comment, upvote), and content management (create, edit, delete). Tools abstract away low-level execution: instead of reasoning about how to click and type, agents simply call search(query) or create(listing). This shifts the computational burden from fragile step-by-step reasoning to reliable tool invocation. On VisualWebArena and WebArena, WALT achieves higher success with fewer steps and less LLM-dependent reasoning, establishing a robust and generalizable paradigm for browser automation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01524","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:51:18Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:51:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:51:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01470","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01470","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01470","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephen","familyName":"Meisenbacher","name":"Meisenbacher, Stephen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Svetlozar","familyName":"Nestorov","name":"Nestorov, Svetlozar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Norlander","name":"Norlander, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Extracting O*NET Features from the NLx Corpus to Build Public Use Aggregate Labor Market Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T21:27:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:11:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Data from online job postings are difficult to access and are not built in a standard or transparent manner. Data included in the standard taxonomy and occupational information database (O*NET) are updated infrequently and based on small survey samples. We adopt O*NET as a framework for building natural language processing tools that extract structured information from job postings. We publish the Job Ad Analysis Toolkit (JAAT), a collection of open-source tools built for this purpose, and demonstrate its reliability and accuracy in out-of-sample and LLM-as-a-Judge testing. We extract more than 10 billion data points from more than 155 million online job ads provided by the National Labor Exchange (NLx) Research Hub, including O*NET tasks, occupation codes, tools, and technologies, as well as wages, skills, industry, and more features. We describe the construction of a dataset of occupation, state, and industry level features aggregated by monthly active jobs from 2015 - 2025. We illustrate the potential for research and future uses in education and workforce development."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"85 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01470","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:49:54Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:49:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:49:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01450","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01450","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01450","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Zuo","name":"Zuo, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhichen","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Zhichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Banghua","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Banghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoran","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhaoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Local Linear Attention: An Optimal Interpolation of Linear and Softmax Attention For Test-Time Regression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T20:42:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:10:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformer architectures have achieved remarkable success in various domains. While efficient alternatives to Softmax Attention have been widely studied, the search for more expressive mechanisms grounded in theoretical insight-even at greater computational cost-has been relatively underexplored. In this work, we bridge this gap by proposing Local Linear Attention (LLA), a novel attention mechanism derived from nonparametric statistics through the lens of test-time regression. First, we show that LLA offers theoretical advantages over Linear and Softmax Attention for associative memory via a bias-variance trade-off analysis. Next, we address its computational challenges and propose two memory-efficient primitives to tackle the $Θ(n^2 d)$ and $Θ(n d^2)$ complexity. We then introduce FlashLLA, a hardware-efficient, blockwise algorithm that enables scalable and parallel computation on modern accelerators. In addition, we implement and profile a customized inference kernel that significantly reduces memory overheads. Finally, we empirically validate the advantages and limitations of LLA on test-time regression, in-context regression, associative recall and state tracking tasks. Experiment results demonstrate that LLA effectively adapts to non-stationarity, outperforming strong baselines in test-time training and in-context learning, and exhibiting promising evidence for its scalability and applicability in large-scale models. Code is available at https://github.com/Yifei-Zuo/Flash-LLA."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01450","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:49:24Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:49:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:49:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01398","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01398","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01398","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zaid","familyName":"Abulawi","name":"Abulawi, Zaid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhiram","familyName":"Garimidi","name":"Garimidi, Abhiram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Doyeong","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Doyeong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Automating Data-Driven Modeling and Analysis for Engineering Applications using Large Language Model Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T19:28:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:07:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern engineering increasingly relies on vast datasets generated by experiments and simulations, driving a growing demand for efficient, reliable, and broadly applicable modeling strategies. There is also heightened interest in developing data-driven approaches, particularly neural network models, for effective prediction and analysis of scientific datasets. Traditional data-driven methods frequently involve extensive manual intervention, limiting their ability to scale effectively and generalize to diverse applications. In this study, we propose an innovative pipeline utilizing Large Language Model (LLM) agents to automate data-driven modeling and analysis, with a particular emphasis on regression tasks. We evaluate two LLM-agent frameworks: a multi-agent system featuring specialized collaborative agents, and a single-agent system based on the Reasoning and Acting (ReAct) paradigm. Both frameworks autonomously handle data preprocessing, neural network development, training, hyperparameter optimization, and uncertainty quantification (UQ). We validate our approach using a critical heat flux (CHF) prediction benchmark, involving approximately 25,000 experimental data points from the OECD/NEA benchmark dataset. Results indicate that our LLM-agent-developed model surpasses traditional CHF lookup tables and delivers predictive accuracy and UQ on par with state-of-the-art Bayesian optimized deep neural network models developed by human experts. These outcomes underscore the significant potential of LLM-based agents to automate complex engineering modeling tasks, greatly reducing human workload while meeting or exceeding existing standards of predictive performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01398","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:48:07Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:48:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:48:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01375","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01375","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01375","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Humaid","familyName":"Ibrahim","name":"Ibrahim, Humaid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolai","familyName":"Rozanov","name":"Rozanov, Nikolai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marek","familyName":"Rei","name":"Rei, Marek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fine-tuning with RAG for Improving LLM Learning of New Skills"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T19:03:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:06:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) agents deployed for multi-step tasks frequently fail in predictable ways: attempting actions with unmet preconditions, issuing redundant commands, or mishandling environment constraints. While retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) can improve performance by providing runtime guidance, it requires maintaining external knowledge databases and adds computational overhead at every deployment. We propose a simple pipeline that converts inference-time retrieval into learned competence through distillation. Our approach: (1) extracts compact, reusable hints from agent failures, (2) uses these hints to generate improved teacher trajectories via one-shot retrieval at episode start, and (3) trains student models on these trajectories with hint strings removed, forcing internalization rather than memorization. Across two interactive benchmarks, ALFWorld (household tasks) and WebShop (online shopping), distilled students consistently outperform baseline agents, achieving up to 91% success on ALFWorld (vs. 79% for baselines) and improving WebShop scores to 72 (vs. 61 for baselines), while using 10-60% fewer tokens than retrieval-augmented teachers depending on the environment. The approach generalizes across model scales (7B/14B parameters) and agent architectures (ReAct/StateAct), demonstrating that retrieval benefits can be effectively internalized through targeted fine-tuning without permanent runtime dependencies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under review at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01375","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:47:34Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:47:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:47:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01363","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01363","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01363","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leon","familyName":"Garza","name":"Garza, Leon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anantaa","familyName":"Kotal","name":"Kotal, Anantaa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael A.","familyName":"Grasso","name":"Grasso, Michael A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emre","familyName":"Umucu","name":"Umucu, Emre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Retrieval-Augmented Framework for LLM-Based Clinical Decision Support"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T18:45:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:05:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The increasing complexity of clinical decision-making, alongside the rapid expansion of electronic health records (EHR), presents both opportunities and challenges for delivering data-informed care. This paper proposes a clinical decision support system powered by Large Language Models (LLMs) to assist prescribing clinicians. The system generates therapeutic suggestions by analyzing historical EHR data, including patient demographics, presenting complaints, clinical symptoms, diagnostic information, and treatment histories. The framework integrates natural language processing with structured clinical inputs to produce contextually relevant recommendations. Rather than replacing clinician judgment, it is designed to augment decision-making by retrieving and synthesizing precedent cases with comparable characteristics, drawing on local datasets or federated sources where applicable. At its core, the system employs a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) pipeline that harmonizes unstructured narratives and codified data to support LLM-based inference. We outline the system's technical components, including representation representation alignment and generation strategies. Preliminary evaluations, conducted with de-identified and synthetic clinical datasets, examine the clinical plausibility and consistency of the model's outputs. Early findings suggest that LLM-based tools may provide valuable decision support in prescribing workflows when appropriately constrained and rigorously validated. This work represents an initial step toward integration of generative AI into real-world clinical decision-making with an emphasis on transparency, safety, and alignment with established practices."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01363","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:47:16Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:47:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:47:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01353","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01353","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01353","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Darshan","familyName":"Deshpande","name":"Deshpande, Darshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Varun","familyName":"Gangal","name":"Gangal, Varun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hersh","familyName":"Mehta","name":"Mehta, Hersh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anand","familyName":"Kannappan","name":"Kannappan, Anand","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rebecca","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Rebecca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MEMTRACK: Evaluating Long-Term Memory and State Tracking in Multi-Platform Dynamic Agent Environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T18:34:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:05:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent works on context and memory benchmarking have primarily focused on conversational instances but the need for evaluating memory in dynamic enterprise environments is crucial for its effective application. We introduce MEMTRACK, a benchmark designed to evaluate long-term memory and state tracking in multi-platform agent environments. MEMTRACK models realistic organizational workflows by integrating asynchronous events across multiple communication and productivity platforms such as Slack, Linear and Git. Each benchmark instance provides a chronologically platform-interleaved timeline, with noisy, conflicting, cross-referring information as well as potential codebase/file-system comprehension and exploration. Consequently, our benchmark tests memory capabilities such as acquistion, selection and conflict resolution. We curate the MEMTRACK dataset through both manual expert driven design and scalable agent based synthesis, generating ecologically valid scenarios grounded in real world software development processes. We introduce pertinent metrics for Correctness, Efficiency, and Redundancy that capture the effectiveness of memory mechanisms beyond simple QA performance. Experiments across SoTA LLMs and memory backends reveal challenges in utilizing memory across long horizons, handling cross-platform dependencies, and resolving contradictions. Notably, the best performing GPT-5 model only achieves a 60\\% Correctness score on MEMTRACK. This work provides an extensible framework for advancing evaluation research for memory-augmented agents, beyond existing focus on conversational setups, and sets the stage for multi-agent, multi-platform memory benchmarking in complex organizational settings"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to NeurIPS 2025 SEA Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01353","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:47:01Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:47:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:47:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01295","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01295","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01295","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zarreen","familyName":"Reza","name":"Reza, Zarreen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Social Laboratory: A Psychometric Framework for Multi-Agent LLM Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T07:10:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:02:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As Large Language Models (LLMs) transition from static tools to autonomous agents, traditional evaluation benchmarks that measure performance on downstream tasks are becoming insufficient. These methods fail to capture the emergent social and cognitive dynamics that arise when agents communicate, persuade, and collaborate in interactive environments. To address this gap, we introduce a novel evaluation framework that uses multi-agent debate as a controlled \"social laboratory\" to discover and quantify these behaviors. In our framework, LLM-based agents, instantiated with distinct personas and incentives, deliberate on a wide range of challenging topics under the supervision of an LLM moderator. Our analysis, enabled by a new suite of psychometric and semantic metrics, reveals several key findings. Across hundreds of debates, we uncover a powerful and robust emergent tendency for agents to seek consensus, consistently reaching high semantic agreement (μ \u0026gt; 0.88) even without explicit instruction and across sensitive topics. We show that assigned personas induce stable, measurable psychometric profiles, particularly in cognitive effort, and that the moderators persona can significantly alter debate outcomes by structuring the environment, a key finding for external AI alignment. This work provides a blueprint for a new class of dynamic, psychometrically grounded evaluation protocols designed for the agentic setting, offering a crucial methodology for understanding and shaping the social behaviors of the next generation of AI agents. We have released the code and results at https://github.com/znreza/multi-agent-LLM-eval-for-debate."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"39th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025) Workshop on Evaluating the Evolving LLM Lifecycle: Benchmarks, Emergent Abilities, and Scaling"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01295","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:45:34Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:45:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:45:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01286","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01286","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01286","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuel","familyName":"Cebrian","name":"Cebrian, Manuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomomi","familyName":"Kito","name":"Kito, Tomomi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raul Castro","familyName":"Fernandez","name":"Fernandez, Raul Castro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Emergent evaluation hubs in a decentralizing large language model ecosystem"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T23:49:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:02:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models are proliferating, and so are the benchmarks that serve as their common yardsticks. We ask how the agglomeration patterns of these two layers compare: do they evolve in tandem or diverge? Drawing on two curated proxies for the ecosystem, the Stanford Foundation-Model Ecosystem Graph and the Evidently AI benchmark registry, we find complementary but contrasting dynamics. Model creation has broadened across countries and organizations and diversified in modality, licensing, and access. Benchmark influence, by contrast, displays centralizing patterns: in the inferred benchmark-author-institution network, the top 15% of nodes account for over 80% of high-betweenness paths, three countries produce 83% of benchmark outputs, and the global Gini for inferred benchmark authority reaches 0.89. An agent-based simulation highlights three mechanisms: higher entry of new benchmarks reduces concentration; rapid inflows can temporarily complicate coordination in evaluation; and stronger penalties against over-fitting have limited effect. Taken together, these results suggest that concentrated benchmark influence functions as coordination infrastructure that supports standardization, comparability, and reproducibility amid rising heterogeneity in model production, while also introducing trade-offs such as path dependence, selective visibility, and diminishing discriminative power as leaderboards saturate."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 11 figures, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01286","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:45:21Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:45:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:45:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01283","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01283","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01283","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Israel Abebe","familyName":"Azime","name":"Azime, Israel Abebe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tadesse Destaw","familyName":"Belay","name":"Belay, Tadesse Destaw","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atnafu Lambebo","familyName":"Tonja","name":"Tonja, Atnafu Lambebo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluation Sheet for Deep Research: A Use Case for Academic Survey Writing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T21:00:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:02:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) powered with argentic capabilities are able to do knowledge-intensive tasks without human involvement. A prime example of this tool is Deep research with the capability to browse the web, extract information and generate multi-page reports. In this work, we introduce an evaluation sheet that can be used for assessing the capability of Deep Research tools. In addition, we selected academic survey writing as a use case task and evaluated output reports based on the evaluation sheet we introduced. Our findings show the need to have carefully crafted evaluation standards. The evaluation done on OpenAI`s Deep Search and Google's Deep Search in generating an academic survey showed the huge gap between search engines and standalone Deep Research tools, the shortcoming in representing the targeted area."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01283","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:45:17Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:45:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:45:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01279","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01279","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01279","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongchao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yongchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiefeng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jiefeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Ji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Na","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Na","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuchu","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Chuchu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomas","familyName":"Pfister","name":"Pfister, Tomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinsung","familyName":"Yoon","name":"Yoon, Jinsung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TUMIX: Multi-Agent Test-Time Scaling with Tool-Use Mixture"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T19:19:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:02:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While integrating tools like Code Interpreter and Search has significantly enhanced Large Language Model (LLM) reasoning in models like ChatGPT Agent and Gemini-Pro, practical guidance on optimal tool use is lacking. The core challenge is effectively combining textual reasoning, coding, and search for diverse questions. In this paper, we propose Tool-Use Mixture (TUMIX), an ensemble framework that runs multiple agents in parallel, each employing distinct tool-use strategies and answer paths. Agents in TUMIX iteratively share and refine responses based on the question and previous answers. In experiments, TUMIX achieves significant gains over state-of-the-art tool-augmented and test-time scaling methods, delivering an average accuracy improvement of up to 3.55% over the best baseline on Gemini-2.5-Pro and Gemini-2.5-Flash across key reasoning benchmarks, with near-equal inference costs. We find that agent diversity and quality are crucial and can be enhanced by using LLMs to auto-optimize agent designs. Furthermore, TUMIX can halt refinement upon reaching sufficient confidence, preserving performance at only 49% of the inference cost. Further scaling can achieve higher performance, albeit at a greater cost."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 13 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01279","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:45:11Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:45:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:45:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01260","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01260","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01260","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ningyuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Ningyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanliang","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Guanliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingchen","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Mingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyi","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihui","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Zhihui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hancheng","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Hancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tingjun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tingjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiran","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"IoT-MCP: Bridging LLMs and IoT Systems Through Model Context Protocol"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-25T08:35:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:01:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) with Internet-of-Things (IoT) systems faces significant challenges in hardware heterogeneity and control complexity. The Model Context Protocol (MCP) emerges as a critical enabler, providing standardized communication between LLMs and physical devices. We propose IoT-MCP, a novel framework that implements MCP through edge-deployed servers to bridge LLMs and IoT ecosystems. To support rigorous evaluation, we introduce IoT-MCP Bench, the first benchmark containing 114 Basic Tasks (e.g., ``What is the current temperature?'') and 1,140 Complex Tasks (e.g., ``I feel so hot, do you have any ideas?'') for IoT-enabled LLMs. Experimental validation across 22 sensor types and 6 microcontroller units demonstrates IoT-MCP's 100% task success rate to generate tool calls that fully meet expectations and obtain completely accurate results, 205ms average response time, and 74KB peak memory footprint. This work delivers both an open-source integration framework (https://github.com/Duke-CEI-Center/IoT-MCP-Servers) and a standardized evaluation methodology for LLM-IoT systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01260","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:44:44Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:44:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:44:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01257","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01257","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01257","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengwang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Zhengwang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ling","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wangqiu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wangqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RJE: A Retrieval-Judgment-Exploration Framework for Efficient Knowledge Graph Question Answering with LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-25T03:56:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:01:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Knowledge graph question answering (KGQA) aims to answer natural language questions using knowledge graphs. Recent research leverages large language models (LLMs) to enhance KGQA reasoning, but faces limitations: retrieval-based methods are constrained by the quality of retrieved information, while agent-based methods rely heavily on proprietary LLMs. To address these limitations, we propose Retrieval-Judgment-Exploration (RJE), a framework that retrieves refined reasoning paths, evaluates their sufficiency, and conditionally explores additional evidence. Moreover, RJE introduces specialized auxiliary modules enabling small-sized LLMs to perform effectively: Reasoning Path Ranking, Question Decomposition, and Retriever-assisted Exploration. Experiments show that our approach with proprietary LLMs (such as GPT-4o-mini) outperforms existing baselines while enabling small open-source LLMs (such as 3B and 8B parameters) to achieve competitive results without fine-tuning LLMs. Additionally, RJE substantially reduces the number of LLM calls and token usage compared to agent-based methods, yielding significant efficiency improvements."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01257","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:44:39Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:44:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:44:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01253","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01253","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01253","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianzhang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jianzhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialong","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jialong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Chuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OR-Toolformer: Modeling and Solving Operations Research Problems with Tool Augmented Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-24T14:42:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:01:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) demonstrate strong mathematical reasoning, but reliance on closed-source APIs for OR tasks raises privacy concerns, and training open-source models from scratch incurs high compute costs. We introduce OR-Toolformer, which fine-tunes Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct with a semi-automatic data synthesis pipeline that generates diverse OR problem-answer pairs and augments the model with external solvers to produce API calls. On three of four standard benchmarks, OR-Toolformer achieves up to 80.1% execution accuracy, exceeding size-matched baselines by over 4.3%. In zero-shot evaluation on two unseen OR problem types, it attains 54% average accuracy, a 21 percentage-point improvement over the strongest baseline. These findings validate the efficacy of tool-augmented fine-tuning LLMs for accurate and generalizable OR problem modeling and solving."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01253","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:44:33Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:44:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:44:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01248","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01248","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01248","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruyue","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ruyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rong","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Rong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangzhen","familyName":"Bo","name":"Bo, Xiangzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoshuai","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Xiaoshuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinwen","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Jinwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiping","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SSTAG: Structure-Aware Self-Supervised Learning Method for Text-Attributed Graphs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-24T09:10:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:01:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large scale pretrained models have revolutionized Natural Language Processing (NLP) and Computer Vision (CV), showcasing remarkable cross domain generalization abilities. However, in graph learning, models are typically trained on individual graph datasets, limiting their capacity to transfer knowledge across different graphs and tasks. This approach also heavily relies on large volumes of annotated data, which presents a significant challenge in resource-constrained settings. Unlike NLP and CV, graph structured data presents unique challenges due to its inherent heterogeneity, including domain specific feature spaces and structural diversity across various applications. To address these challenges, we propose a novel structure aware self supervised learning method for Text Attributed Graphs (SSTAG). By leveraging text as a unified representation medium for graph learning, SSTAG bridges the gap between the semantic reasoning of Large Language Models (LLMs) and the structural modeling capabilities of Graph Neural Networks (GNNs). Our approach introduces a dual knowledge distillation framework that co-distills both LLMs and GNNs into structure-aware multilayer perceptrons (MLPs), enhancing the scalability of large-scale TAGs. Additionally, we introduce an in-memory mechanism that stores typical graph representations, aligning them with memory anchors in an in-memory repository to integrate invariant knowledge, thereby improving the model's generalization ability. Extensive experiments demonstrate that SSTAG outperforms state-of-the-art models on cross-domain transfer learning tasks, achieves exceptional scalability, and reduces inference costs while maintaining competitive performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01248","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:44:26Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:44:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:44:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01246","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01246","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01246","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqing","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Jiaqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Comparative Analysis of Sparse Autoencoder and Activation Difference in Language Model Steering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-24T08:31:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:01:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sparse autoencoders (SAEs) have recently emerged as a powerful tool for language model steering. Prior work has explored top-k SAE latents for steering, but we observe that many dimensions among the top-k latents capture non-semantic features such as punctuation rather than semantic attributes like instructions. To address this, we propose focusing on a single, most relevant SAE latent (top-1), eliminating redundant features. We further identify a limitation in constant SAE steering, which often produces degenerate outputs such as repetitive single words. To mitigate this, we introduce a token-wise decaying steering strategy, enabling more faithful comparisons with mean activation difference baselines. Empirically, we show that steering an SAE latent associated with reasoning reliably elicits step-by-step mathematical reasoning and enhances inference quality, functionally resembling the effect of appending a guiding token. Our results demonstrate that SAEs outperform mean activation difference methods on mathematical reasoning benchmarks and match their performance on IF-Eval."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01246","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:44:23Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:44:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:44:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01245","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01245","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01245","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runfei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Runfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuyang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Shuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SeMob: Semantic Synthesis for Dynamic Urban Mobility Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-24T05:33:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:01:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Human mobility prediction is vital for urban services, but often fails to account for abrupt changes from external events. Existing spatiotemporal models struggle to leverage textual descriptions detailing these events. We propose SeMob, an LLM-powered semantic synthesis pipeline for dynamic mobility prediction. Specifically, SeMob employs a multi-agent framework where LLM-based agents automatically extract and reason about spatiotemporally related text from complex online texts. Fine-grained relevant contexts are then incorporated with spatiotemporal data through our proposed innovative progressive fusion architecture. The rich pre-trained event prior contributes enriched insights about event-driven prediction, and hence results in a more aligned forecasting model. Evaluated on a dataset constructed through our pipeline, SeMob achieves maximal reductions of 13.92% in MAE and 11.12% in RMSE compared to the spatiotemporal model. Notably, the framework exhibits pronounced superiority especially within spatiotemporal regions close to an event's location and time of occurrence."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01245","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:44:21Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:44:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:44:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01244","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01244","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01244","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyeoneui","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyeoneui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeongha","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jeongha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huijing","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Huijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinsun","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Jinsun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunghoon","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Sunghoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sun Joo","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Sun Joo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Feasibility of Structuring Stress Documentation Using an Ontology-Guided Large Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-24T04:10:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:01:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Stress, arising from the dynamic interaction between external stressors, individual appraisals, and physiological or psychological responses, significantly impacts health yet is often underreported and inconsistently documented, typically captured as unstructured free-text in electronic health records. Ambient AI technologies offer promise in reducing documentation burden, but predominantly generate unstructured narratives, limiting downstream clinical utility.\n This study aimed to develop an ontology for mental stress and evaluate the feasibility of using a Large Language Model (LLM) to extract ontology-guided stress-related information from narrative text. The Mental Stress Ontology (MeSO) was developed by integrating theoretical models like the Transactional Model of Stress with concepts from 11 validated stress assessment tools. MeSO's structure and content were refined using Ontology Pitfall Scanner! and expert validation.\n Using MeSO, six categories of stress-related information--stressor, stress response, coping strategy, duration, onset, and temporal profile--were extracted from 35 Reddit posts using Claude Sonnet 4. Human reviewers evaluated accuracy and ontology coverage. The final ontology included 181 concepts across eight top-level classes. Of 220 extractable stress-related items, the LLM correctly identified 172 (78.2%), misclassified 27 (12.3%), and missed 21 (9.5%). All correctly extracted items were accurately mapped to MeSO, although 24 relevant concepts were not yet represented in the ontology.\n This study demonstrates the feasibility of using an ontology-guided LLM for structured extraction of stress-related information, offering potential to enhance the consistency and utility of stress documentation in ambient AI systems. Future work should involve clinical dialogue data and comparison across LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01244","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:44:20Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:44:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:44:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01237","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01237","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01237","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nandakishor","familyName":"M","name":"M, Nandakishor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Confidence-Aware Routing for Large Language Model Reliability Enhancement: A Multi-Signal Approach to Pre-Generation Hallucination Mitigation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-23T18:34:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:01:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models suffer from hallucination, generating plausible yet factually incorrect content. Current mitigation strategies focus on post-generation correction, which is computationally expensive and fails to prevent unreliable content generation. We propose a confidence-aware routing system that proactively assesses model uncertainty before generation and redirects queries based on estimated reliability. Our approach combines three complementary signals: semantic alignment between internal representations and reference embeddings, internal convergence analysis across model layers, and learned confidence estimation. The unified confidence score determines routing to four pathways: local generation for high confidence, retrieval-augmented generation for medium confidence, larger models for low confidence, and human review for very low confidence. Evaluation on knowledge-intensive QA benchmarks demonstrates significant improvements in hallucination detection (0.74 vs. 0.42 baseline) while reducing computational costs by 40% compared to post-hoc methods. The F1 score improves from 0.61 to 0.82 with low false positive rates (0.09). This paradigm shift from reactive correction to proactive assessment offers a computationally efficient approach to LLM reliability enhancement."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01237","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:44:10Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:44:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:44:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01232","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01232","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01232","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongjun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Dongjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gyuho","familyName":"Shim","name":"Shim, Gyuho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongchan","familyName":"Chun","name":"Chun, Yongchan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minhyuk","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Minhyuk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chanjun","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Chanjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heuiseok","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Heuiseok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Benchmark Profiling: Mechanistic Diagnosis of LLM Benchmarks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-23T15:32:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:01:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models are commonly judged by their scores on standard benchmarks, yet such scores often overstate real capability since they mask the mix of skills a task actually demands. For example, ARC is assumed to test reasoning, while HellaSwag is designed to evaluate commonsense. However, we lack a systematic way to verify if these benchmarks actually measure these labels. We introduce Benchmark Profiling, a diagnostic framework that decomposes benchmark performance into ten cognitively grounded abilities. The method combines gradient-based importance scoring with targeted parameter ablation to compute an Ability Impact Score (AIS) that quantifies how much each ability contributes to a model's success on a given benchmark. Profiling three instruction-tuned models across ten widely used benchmarks yields four key findings: (i) most benchmarks draw on several abilities rather than one, (ii) datasets with similar labels rely on distinct ability mixtures, (iii) code-generation benchmarks reward broad, multi-skill improvement and thus show only modest gains from narrow domain-specific fine-tuning, and (iv) abilities irrelevant to the task could negatively affect performance. Benchmark Profiling therefore explains why performance gains do not always translate into user-perceived competence and offers a transparent tool for benchmark audit and model interpretability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 5 figures. Accepted to EMNLP 2025 main conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01232","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:44:03Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:44:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:44:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01226","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01226","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01226","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akshith Reddy","familyName":"Putta","name":"Putta, Akshith Reddy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob","familyName":"Devasier","name":"Devasier, Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengkai","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chengkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ClaimCheck: Real-Time Fact-Checking with Small Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T21:18:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:01:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce ClaimCheck, an LLM-guided automatic fact-checking system designed to verify real-world claims using live Web evidence and small language models. Unlike prior systems that rely on large, closed-source models and static knowledge stores, ClaimCheck employs a transparent, stepwise verification pipeline that mirrors human fact-checking workflows consisting of Web search query planning, Web-based evidence retrieval and summarization, evidence synthesis and re-retrieval, and claim verdict evaluation. Each module is optimized for small LLMs, allowing the system to deliver accurate and interpretable fact-checking with significantly lower computational requirements. Despite using a much smaller Qwen3-4B model, ClaimCheck achieves state-of-the-art accuracy of 76.4% on the AVeriTeC dataset, outperforming previous approaches using LLaMA3.1 70B and GPT-4o. Extensive ablations demonstrate that careful modular design and prompting strategies can overcome the limitations of smaller LLMs. To promote accessibility and transparency, we provide a public demo at https://idir.uta.edu/claimcheck."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01226","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:43:54Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:43:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:43:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01224","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01224","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01224","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tyler J","familyName":"Poore","name":"Poore, Tyler J","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher J","familyName":"Pinard","name":"Pinard, Christopher J","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aleena","familyName":"Shabbir","name":"Shabbir, Aleena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Lagree","name":"Lagree, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andre","familyName":"Telfer","name":"Telfer, Andre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuan-Chuen","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Kuan-Chuen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Context Matters: Comparison of commercial large language tools in veterinary medicine"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T18:52:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:00:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used in clinical settings, yet their performance in veterinary medicine remains underexplored. We evaluated three commercially available veterinary-focused LLM summarization tools (Product 1 [Hachiko] and Products 2 and 3) on a standardized dataset of veterinary oncology records. Using a rubric-guided LLM-as-a-judge framework, summaries were scored across five domains: Factual Accuracy, Completeness, Chronological Order, Clinical Relevance, and Organization. Product 1 achieved the highest overall performance, with a median average score of 4.61 (IQR: 0.73), compared to 2.55 (IQR: 0.78) for Product 2 and 2.45 (IQR: 0.92) for Product 3. It also received perfect median scores in Factual Accuracy and Chronological Order. To assess the internal consistency of the grading framework itself, we repeated the evaluation across three independent runs. The LLM grader demonstrated high reproducibility, with Average Score standard deviations of 0.015 (Product 1), 0.088 (Product 2), and 0.034 (Product 3). These findings highlight the importance of veterinary-specific commercial LLM tools and demonstrate that LLM-as-a-judge evaluation is a scalable and reproducible method for assessing clinical NLP summarization in veterinary medicine."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"4 Figures, 10 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01224","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:43:51Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:43:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:43:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01197","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01197","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01197","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gadir","familyName":"Suleymanli","name":"Suleymanli, Gadir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Rogiers","name":"Rogiers, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucas","familyName":"Lageweg","name":"Lageweg, Lucas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jefrey","familyName":"Lijffijt","name":"Lijffijt, Jefrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are LLMs ready to help non-expert users to make charts of official statistics data?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-03T08:11:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:00:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this time when biased information, deep fakes, and propaganda proliferate, the accessibility of reliable data sources is more important than ever. National statistical institutes provide curated data that contain quantitative information on a wide range of topics. However, that information is typically spread across many tables and the plain numbers may be arduous to process. Hence, this open data may be practically inaccessible. We ask the question \"Are current Generative AI models capable of facilitating the identification of the right data and the fully-automatic creation of charts to provide information in visual form, corresponding to user queries?\". We present a structured evaluation of recent large language models' (LLMs) capabilities to generate charts from complex data in response to user queries. Working with diverse public data from Statistics Netherlands, we assessed multiple LLMs on their ability to identify relevant data tables, perform necessary manipulations, and generate appropriate visualizations autonomously. We propose a new evaluation framework spanning three dimensions: data retrieval \u0026amp; pre-processing, code quality, and visual representation. Results indicate that locating and processing the correct data represents the most significant challenge. Additionally, LLMs rarely implement visualization best practices without explicit guidance. When supplemented with information about effective chart design, models showed marked improvement in representation scores. Furthermore, an agentic approach with iterative self-evaluation led to excellent performance across all evaluation dimensions. These findings suggest that LLMs' effectiveness for automated chart generation can be enhanced through appropriate scaffolding and feedback mechanisms, and that systems can already reach the necessary accuracy across the three evaluation dimensions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01197","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:43:12Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:43:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:43:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":84,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-02T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-03T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28subjects.subject%3A%22cs.AI%22+OR+subjects.subject%3A%22cs.IR%22+OR+subjects.subject%3A%22cs.MA%22+OR+subjects.subject%3A%22cs.CL%22%29+AND+%28titles.title%3A%22language+model%22+OR+titles.title%3A%22language+models%22+OR+titles.title%3A%22LLM%22+OR+titles.title%3A%22LLMs%22+OR+titles.title%3A%22transformer%22+OR+titles.title%3A%22transformers%22+OR+titles.title%3A%22foundation+model%22+OR+titles.title%3A%22foundation+models%22+OR+descriptions.description%3A%22language+model%22+OR+descriptions.description%3A%22language+models%22+OR+descriptions.description%3A%22LLM%22+OR+descriptions.description%3A%22LLMs%22+OR+descriptions.description%3A%22transformer%22+OR+descriptions.description%3A%22transformers%22+OR+descriptions.description%3A%